= NTFS
{parent=File system}
{c}
{wiki}

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Inode
{parent=File system}
{wiki}

An <inode> is a <data structure> describing an object in an <inode>-based <file system>. For an ordinary <file>, it records <file metadata> such as type, owner, access permissions, size and <hard link> count, together with information locating the contents. In the direct-reference toy layout, <inode> 42 points to <file data blocks> 7 and 9; its two names `a` and `b` live in <directory entries>. Other <file systems> can locate contents differently, so the diagram is a model of the separation rather than a universal storage layout. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/file-implementation.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 40, sections 40.3–40.4: inodes and directory organization]{ref}

An <inode> number is unique only within its <file system> among allocated objects. Two different <file systems> can each have <inode> 42, referring to unrelated <files>. <POSIX> exposes the <file system>'s device identity as `st_dev` and the <file>'s serial number as `st_ino`; the pair identifies a <file> within a running system. Neither the bare <inode> number nor that pair is a permanent historical identifier: after reclamation, a later <file> can reuse the number. In particular, comparing an old saved number with a new one does not establish continuity of the <file>. https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/basedefs/sys_stat.h.html[The Open Group, POSIX.1-2017, sys/stat.h: joint identity from st_dev and st_ino]{ref}

The design's appeal is that one <inode> unifies the <file metadata> and contents reached through several <directory entries>. Ritchie and Thompson's original 1974 account explicitly describes a <directory entry> as a name and an i-number, with the corresponding <inode> holding ownership, protection, size, content addresses and the <hard link> count. It gives a concrete early explanation of how names and <file> identity can evolve separately. https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~15712/papers/ritchie74.pdf[Ritchie and Thompson, The UNIX Time-Sharing System, 1974, section 4: implementation of the file system]{ref}

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Synchronous processor exception
{parent=Operating system}

A processor event attributable to instruction execution, such as a <page fault> during a memory access or a divide exception on <x86>. This causal connection distinguishes it from the asynchronous device <interrupts> discussed here. It also differs from a programming language's <exception handling>, which specifies how program-level exceptions propagate. https://docs.kernel.org/arch/x86/entry_64.html[Linux kernel documentation, Kernel Entries: architecturally defined exceptions]{ref}

= Device driver
{parent=Operating system}
{wiki}

Software supplying device-specific operations behind an <operating system> interface. For these examples its responsibilities include <DMA> setup and completion handling, or checking status for <polled input-output>. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/file-devices.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 36, section 36.7: the driver abstraction]{ref}

= Interrupt
{parent=Operating system}
{wiki}

An <interrupt> redirects <CPU> execution to an <interrupt handler> in response to an event. Here the main case is an asynchronous hardware <interrupt>: a timer or device requests attention independently of the instruction currently being executed. A device completing a receive operation can announce that its data is ready while the <CPU> is running another program. <Interrupt-driven input-output> illustrates this with an explicit timeline. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/cpu-mechanisms.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 6, section 6.3: timer interrupts and regaining control]{ref}

A <synchronous processor exception>, such as a <page fault> caused by a particular memory access, is tied to instruction execution. A <system call> is an intentional program request for an <operating-system kernel> service. These routes can share entry machinery, and an instruction named a software interrupt can implement a <system call>, but this does not make that request an asynchronous device event. For example, the <Linux kernel> on <x86> documents separate entries for external <interrupts>, divide exceptions, `syscall` and `int 0x80`. https://docs.kernel.org/arch/x86/entry_64.html[Linux kernel documentation, Kernel Entries: interrupt, exception and system-call entry points]{ref}

The usual device route is a request through an <interrupt controller>, architecture-specific entry, and dispatch to an <interrupt handler>. In the <Linux kernel>, generic handling separates controller operations from flow handling and <device driver> callbacks. Requests can arrive through a pin or a message, and several devices can share a request source. Routing, priorities and <interrupt masking> determine which requests can be delivered; notification therefore need not mean immediate execution of the <interrupt handler>. https://docs.kernel.org/core-api/genericirq.html[Linux kernel documentation, generic IRQ handling: abstraction layers and interrupt control flow]{ref}https://docs.kernel.org/core-api/irq/concepts.html[Linux kernel documentation, What is an IRQ?: pin and packet requests, shared sources]{ref}

The <CPU> and entry code preserve the state needed to resume interrupted execution, including the <instruction pointer>. Handling may enter a higher <processor privilege level> when interrupting ordinary program execution, but an <interrupt> can also arrive while privileged code is already running. The protected entry route and handler placement depend on the architecture and <operating system>; a <microkernel> need not place all <device driver> work where a <monolithic kernel> does. An <interrupt> entry is also distinct from a <context switch> to another <operating-system process>. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/cpu-mechanisms.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 6, sections 6.2–6.3: protected entry, saved state and switching]{ref}https://docs.kernel.org/arch/x86/entry_64.html[Linux kernel documentation, Kernel Entries: entry from user mode and already executing kernel code]{ref}

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Interrupt-driven input-output
{parent=Interrupt}

In <interrupt-driven input-output>, a device signals an <interrupt> when attention is needed, letting the <CPU> avoid repeatedly checking an idle device. The <interrupt handler> determines what happened and arranges the required work; notification itself does not move the payload. <DMA> can supply that separate data-transfer mechanism.

For a toy comparison, let events occur at times $2,5,9$. Ideal <interrupt> notification happens at those same times, while <polled input-output> at $0,2,4,6,8,10,12$ detects them at $2,6,10$. The polling delays are $0,1,1$; ideal notification delays are all zero. Events are assumed to remain pending, with a simultaneous event visible to a poll. The chart is a model, not a measurement: it excludes <interrupt> delivery and dispatch latency, <interrupt handler> service time, and polling overhead. Its zero-delay row is not a real-hardware guarantee.

\Image[interrupt-versus-polling-timeline.png]
{title=Ideal <interrupt> notification versus periodic <polled input-output> for three persistent device events}

The useful idea is to spend attention when something changes. Sparse events can leave long stretches for useful <CPU> work instead of busy checks. At high event rates, repeated entry and service overhead can consume that saving. Batching notifications can reduce their frequency while delaying some events; switching to <polled input-output> during active periods is another option. No strategy is universally faster. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/file-devices.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 36, section 36.4: interrupt costs and coalescing]{ref}

The <Linux kernel>'s NAPI mechanism is a concrete combination: an <interrupt> normally schedules event processing, which polls for work with a receive budget. Further <interrupts> remain masked during that polling, and are enabled again when polling finishes. NAPI also supports busy polling; the exact processing context and configuration vary. This is a documented <Linux kernel> design, not a requirement on every <operating system>. https://docs.kernel.org/networking/napi.html[Linux kernel documentation, NAPI: basic operation, datapath API, scheduling and IRQ masking]{ref}

= Interrupt handler
{parent=Interrupt}
{wiki}

Code entered to handle an <interrupt>. It identifies and acknowledges relevant device state and arranges service, possibly deferring work. The <Linux kernel>'s generic flow layer and <device driver> callback have distinct responsibilities. https://docs.kernel.org/core-api/genericirq.html[Linux kernel documentation, generic IRQ handling: interrupt control flow and default flow implementations]{ref}

= Interrupt controller
{parent=Interrupt}

Hardware managing and routing <interrupt> requests to a <CPU>, with architecture-dependent masking and priority rules. The <Linux kernel> encapsulates its operations separately from generic flow handlers. https://docs.kernel.org/core-api/genericirq.html[Linux kernel documentation, generic IRQ handling: chip-level hardware encapsulation]{ref}

// Destination: computer-science.bigb

= Interrupt masking
{parent=Interrupt}

Disabling selected asynchronous interrupt delivery, normally under privileged control. It does not necessarily disable processor exceptions, nonmaskable interrupts or activity on other processors.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Processor privilege level
{parent=Operating system}

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Multics
{parent=Operating system}
{c}
{wiki}

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Multiprogramming
{parent=Operating system}
{wiki}

Keeping several <operating-system processes> available so <process scheduling> can run another when one waits. Increasing their number can increase the combined <working set> demand beyond the available <page frames>, producing <memory thrashing> rather than more useful <CPU> work.

= Virtual memory
{parent=Operating system}
{wiki}

<Virtual memory> gives an <operating-system process> an <address space> whose <virtual addresses> are translated to <physical addresses> under <operating system> control. In a system using <paging>, each resident <memory page> maps to a <page frame>; <page tables> describe the mappings and access permissions, while the <memory management unit> enforces them. The same <virtual address> in two <operating-system processes> can refer to different <page frames>. A deliberate shared mapping can instead make different <virtual addresses> refer to the same <page frame>. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/vm-intro.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 13: Address Spaces]{ref}

For example, with 256-<byte> <memory pages>, let process A map virtual page `0x12` to <page frame> `0x07`, and process B map virtual page `0x12` to <page frame> `0x09`. Their identical <virtual address> `0x1234` then denotes <physical address> `0x0734` in A and `0x0934` in B. The address acquires meaning from the current <address space>, not just its numerical value. See <paging> for the calculation.

This separation makes programs easier to place in <computer memory>: consecutive <virtual addresses> need not occupy consecutive <page frames>. It also provides isolation and controlled sharing. <Virtual memory> does not require secondary storage: a system can translate and protect <virtual addresses> while keeping every allocated <memory page> resident in <computer memory>. <Demand paging> adds allocation or loading on first use, and can support an <address space> larger than available <computer memory>; the amount that can be used at once is still limited. Keeping a <working set> resident avoids <memory thrashing>.

An early concrete implementation was Manchester's Atlas. Kilburn, Edwards, Lanigan and Sumner's April 1962 paper describes automatically combining fast core storage with drum storage, transferring 512-word blocks and translating their addresses through page address registers. Its attraction was to spare programmers explicit transfers between these storage levels. This historical use of secondary storage is one application of <virtual memory>, rather than a requirement of every <virtual memory> system. The accessible text below is a reprint of the paper. https://www.dcs.gla.ac.uk/~wpc/grcs/kilburn.pdf[Kilburn et al., One-Level Storage System, 1962, sections 1 and 3]{ref}

= Address space
{parent=Virtual memory}
{wiki}

The collection of addresses interpreted in a particular context. An <operating-system process>'s <address space> includes addresses that may be unmapped or inaccessible; belonging to its numerical range does not imply permission to access them.

= Virtual address
{parent=Address space}

An address interpreted within an <address space>, before <address translation> determines the corresponding <physical address>.

= Address translation
{parent=Virtual memory}

Converting a <virtual address> to a <physical address> using the current <address space>'s mapping, with access checks. In <paging>, the <memory page> number changes while the offset within it stays fixed.

<Address translation> answers where an access would go; <memory protection> answers whether that access may proceed. Neither answer is the stored value. Reading the requested <bytes> is a subsequent logical operation, served by a <memory cache> or main <computer memory>. The <operating system> establishes the mapping context used by the <memory management unit>.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Mechanism: Address Translation>[Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces, chapter 15, Mechanism: Address Translation, printed pp. 1–2: hardware mechanism and operating-system control]{ref}

Consider an original toy model with unsigned 12-<bit> <virtual addresses>, <byte> addressing, and 64-<byte> <memory pages> and <page frames>. Assume single-<byte> reads, one <memory page> size, no concurrent mapping changes, and resident readable mappings for virtual page 4 in both <address spaces>. The <virtual address> $v=\mathtt{0x12D}=301$ has six page-number <bits> and six offset <bits>, rather than a split between <hexadecimal> digits. By <Euclidean division>,
$$
301=4\cdot64+45,\qquad q=4,\qquad d=45=\mathtt{0x2D}.
$$
Process A maps virtual page 4 to <page frame> 13; process B maps it to <page frame> 9. Thus the identical <virtual address> denotes
$$
a_A=13\cdot64+45=877=\mathtt{0x36D},\qquad a_B=9\cdot64+45=621=\mathtt{0x26D}.
$$
These are addresses, not the <bytes> read there. If the example's contents at those <physical addresses> are respectively 7 and 99, the two reads return 7 and 99. The mapping calculation alone cannot determine either value.

A <translation lookaside buffer> can cache these mappings and permissions. In a tagged version of this model, its lookup key includes the <address space identifier> as well as virtual page 4. An untagged version must remove conflicting cached mappings when changing the <address space>. Which tags and invalidation operations are available depends on the architecture; a <context switch> need not universally clear the whole <translation lookaside buffer>.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Paging: Faster Translations (TLBs)>[Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces, chapter 19, Paging: Faster Translations (TLBs), section 19.5, printed pp. 9–10: flushing and address-space identifiers]{ref}

The arithmetic explains the beauty of <paging>: a mapping can move an entire <memory page> without changing its internal positions. Indeed, for two offsets $d_1,d_2$ in the same mapped <memory page>,
$$
(fP+d_2)-(fP+d_1)=d_2-d_1.
$$
For process A, virtual page 4 spans `0x100`–`0x13F` and maps to `0x340`–`0x37F`. Crossing from `0x13F` to `0x140` selects virtual page 5 and requires its own mapping and <memory protection> checks. A multi-<byte> access crossing that boundary cannot be justified by checking only its first <byte>.

= Memory management unit
{parent=Virtual memory}
{wiki}
{title2=MMU}

The hardware that performs <address translation> and access checks. Whether a <TLB miss> is resolved entirely by hardware or with <operating system> assistance depends on the architecture.

For <paging>, the <memory management unit> uses the active <address space> and the kind of access: reading, writing, or fetching an instruction. <Processor privilege level> can also affect permission. A <TLB hit> supplies cached translation attributes; it does not bypass <memory protection>. On a <TLB miss>, some architectures perform a hardware <page table walk>, while others enter an <operating system> refill handler. Both can resolve a resident mapping without a nonresident <page fault>.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Paging: Faster Translations (TLBs)>[Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces, chapter 19, Paging: Faster Translations (TLBs), sections 19.1, 19.3–19.4, printed pp. 2, 6, 8: permission checks, refill ownership, and cached attributes]{ref}

\Image[address-translation-tlb-control-path.png]
{title=Logical <address translation> control path: a cached mapping, refill, permission check, and separate data read}

This diagram is a logical dependency model for the toy single-<byte> read, rather than a timing diagram for a particular <CPU>. A <translation lookaside buffer> caches translations and access attributes; a data <memory cache> caches contents. A <TLB hit> can accompany a data <cache miss>, and a <TLB miss> can accompany a data <cache hit> after successful refill. Reading <page table entries> during refill is also distinct from reading the requested data. Actual implementations may overlap steps.

Extend process A's toy <address space> with these independent starting states. Virtual page 4 is resident in <page frame> 13 and readable but not writable. Virtual page 5 is a permitted readable <memory page> whose old contents are in backing storage and which is initially nonresident. Virtual page 6 has no mapping authorized by the <operating system>. Assume permission changes and eviction always invalidate affected cached translations before later accesses, no <copy-on-write> policy, and a free <page frame> 2 for loading virtual page 5.

* Read `0x12D` with an empty <translation lookaside buffer>: a <TLB miss> discovers resident virtual page 4, caches its attributes, and reads <physical address> `0x36D`. There is no nonresident <page fault>.
* Write `0x12D` with that translation already cached: a <TLB hit> finds the mapping, but <memory protection> denies the write. The target <byte> remains unchanged even though its <page frame> is resident.
* Read `0x16D`: virtual page 5 and offset 45 are permitted but nonresident. A <page fault> lets the <operating system> load the contents into <page frame> 2, update the <page table>, and retry. Successful <address translation> then gives $2\cdot64+45=173=\mathtt{0x0AD}$; the requested read occurs only after the contents are ready.
* Read `0x1AD`: virtual page 6 and offset 45 have no authorized mapping. The <operating system> rejects the access in this model; finding a free <page frame> would not supply authorization.

\Image[memory-access-permission-residency-traces.png]
{title=Four original access traces distinguish <TLB misses>, denied writes, nonresident <memory pages>, and unauthorized addresses}

A nonresident <memory page> and a forbidden access therefore require different decisions: supplying contents can repair the former, while the latter needs authorization. Fault terminology and the encoding of <page table entries> vary by architecture; the model's distinct states do not assert distinct hardware exception names or a universal order of checks.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Beyond Physical Memory: Mechanisms>[Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces, chapter 21, Beyond Physical Memory: Mechanisms, sections 21.2–21.5, printed pp. 3–7, especially the terminology aside and Figures 21.2–21.3]{ref}

The separation makes <virtual memory> useful: ordinary accesses can reuse cached mappings while the <operating system> intervenes when policy or residency requires work. It also makes failures easier to diagnose. A lost cached translation, unavailable contents, and denied permission are three different obstacles, even when all interrupt the same requested read.

= Memory protection
{parent=Virtual memory}
{wiki}

Rules controlling access to <computer memory>. In <paging>, <page table entries> and the <memory management unit> enforce permissions; <copy-on-write> temporarily denies writes to logically writable private <memory pages> so a write enters <copy-on-write fault handling>.

= Copy-on-write
{parent=Virtual memory}
{wiki}

<Copy-on-write> defers copying until a modification needs private storage. With <paging>, separate <address spaces> can map their <memory pages> to the same <page frames>, using <memory protection> to prevent writes to the common contents. Reads need no copy. A permitted write to a shared <page frame> first separates the writer's storage, so the other <operating-system process> keeps the old contents. Sharing is safe because the common contents remain unchanged while shared; the logical private copies can still evolve independently.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Complete Virtual Memory Systems>[pp. 7–8: protected sharing, copying on a write, and fork/exec motivation]{ref}

Consider a <fork (system call)>-like model with parent A and child B. Each has a separate <page table>, with three inherited private, logically writable <memory pages>. Both initially map virtual page numbers `0x12`, `0x13`, `0x14` to <page frames> F0, F1, F2. The <page table entries> temporarily deny writes for <copy-on-write>; a read of `x` at offset `0x34` in virtual page `0x12` returns 7 in either <address space>. Separate <page tables> do not imply separate stored <bytes>.

\Image[copy-on-write-shared-page-mappings.png]
{title=Before a write: two <page tables> map six <memory pages> to three shared <page frames>}

Now B writes `x = 42`. In this model, <copy-on-write fault handling> allocates F3, copies all of F0's contents into F3, redirects only B's <page table entry>, enables its write, and retries the store. A still reads 7 through F0; B reads 42 through F3 at the same <virtual address>. F1 and F2 remain shared. The <reference counting> labels count mappings in this example: F0 drops from two to one, while F3 starts at one. Updating one <byte> can therefore copy a whole <memory page>.

\Image[copy-on-write-private-page-after-write.png]
{title=After B writes: one new <page frame>, one whole-page copy, and independent contents at the same <virtual address>}

The attraction is avoiding work that may never be needed. If B only reads, this model makes no data copies; if B replaces its program and discards these mappings, copying them beforehand would have been wasted. <Copy-on-write> trades eager copying for protected sharing and later <page faults>. The <page tables>, <reference counting>, and <operating-system process> records still cost time and <computer memory>.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Complete Virtual Memory Systems>[pp. 7–8: a subsequent exec can discard most of the inherited address space]{ref}

For a concrete <operating system> contract, the `fork(2)` manual for the <Linux kernel> documents <copy-on-write>, while its `mmap(2)` manual distinguishes `MAP_PRIVATE` from `MAP_SHARED`. Writes through `MAP_PRIVATE` are not propagated to other mappings or the underlying <file>; writes through `MAP_SHARED` are visible to other <operating-system processes> mapping that region. An inherited shared mapping therefore retains shared-write behavior. For a private <memory-mapped file>, visibility of later changes to the underlying <file> is unspecified: private writes alone do not guarantee a frozen snapshot of the <file>. <fork(2) — Linux manual page>{ref}<mmap(2) — Linux manual page>{ref}

Nor does <fork (system call)> make every resource independent. With the <Linux kernel>, inherited <file descriptors> refer to the same <open file descriptions>, including shared file positions. Special mapping policies can omit a region from inheritance or zero it in the child. Thus the diagrams describe ordinary inherited private <memory pages>, not every resource reachable by a parent. <fork(2) — Linux manual page>{ref}

= Copy-on-write fault handling
{parent=Copy-on-write}

The <operating-system kernel> must distinguish a temporarily protected <copy-on-write> mapping from one whose writes are forbidden. The teaching procedure in MIT's xv6 lab protects both <page tables>, copies the shared <page frame> on a valid write <page fault>, and installs a writable <page table entry>. Originally read-only code remains read-only. Its <reference counting> increments on sharing and decrements on removal; storage is freed only after the last reference disappears. The lab also requires kernel writes into user storage to respect <copy-on-write>, and specifies failure if no free storage remains. <6.1810  and  Fall 2022/Lab: Copy-on-Write Fork for xv6>{ref}

Every write must respect the sharing policy, but every write need not copy. Repeated writes to B's already private writable F3 need no further <copy-on-write> <page fault>. If A later writes F0, an implementation can reuse F0 when it establishes exclusive, writable ownership; A's remaining read-only <page table entry> may still need a <page fault> to enable the write. This exclusive-reuse rule is an assumption of <copy-on-write page accounting> below, not a promise that every <operating system> uses that path. A count of one alone is not a universal permission to alter storage belonging to a <memory-mapped file> or another owner.

Changing mappings or permissions must also make cached <address translations> consistent. <Cache and TLB Flushing Under Linux by David S. Miller>{ref} Concurrent <software threads> require <synchronization> around ownership checks, copying, and installation to prevent a <race condition>; neither diagrams nor counts specify a production <operating-system kernel>'s locking or hardware invalidation protocol.

= Copy-on-write page accounting
{parent=Copy-on-write}

Assume exactly two <operating-system processes>, $N$ initially resident private <memory pages> of $P$ <bytes> each, initially distinct <page frames> shared by A and B. Neither unmaps, exits, forks again, or encounters <page replacement>; no other owner exists, allocation succeeds, and a first write copies one whole shared <memory page>. Once a <page frame> is exclusively owned it is reused on writes, without another copy; writable mappings remain writable. Count data storage only, excluding <page tables> and other bookkeeping. With B as the only writer, writing $k$ distinct <memory pages> gives
$$
\text{new allocations}=\text{whole-page copies}=k,\qquad \text{resident data bytes}=(N+k)P.
$$
This depends on distinct written <memory pages>, not the number of store instructions or reads. For the diagrams, take $N=3$ and an illustrative $P=4096$ <bytes>:

\Table[
\Tr[\Th[Access pattern]\Th[New <page frames>]\Th[Whole-page copies]\Th[Resident data <bytes>]]
\Tr[\Td[Both only read]\Td[0]\Td[0]\Td[12288]]
\Tr[\Td[B writes <memory page> `0x12` once]\Td[1]\Td[1]\Td[16384]]
\Tr[\Td[B writes <memory page> `0x12` one hundred times]\Td[1]\Td[1]\Td[16384]]
\Tr[\Td[B writes <memory pages> `0x12` and `0x14`]\Td[2]\Td[2]\Td[20480]]
\Tr[\Td[B writes all three <memory pages>]\Td[3]\Td[3]\Td[24576]]
\Tr[\Td[B then A each write <memory page> `0x12`]\Td[1]\Td[1]\Td[16384]]
]

For both writers, let $S_A,S_B$ be the <sets> of initial <memory page> numbers written by A and B. Under these assumptions, new allocations and copies both equal $|S_A\cup S_B|$, the <cardinality> of their <set union>, while resident data storage is $(N+|S_A\cup S_B|)P$. Each initially shared <page frame> needs at most one split: after the first writer copies it, the other owns the original exclusively. Both writing the same <memory page> therefore produces two versions with one copy, even if the final values happen to match. The last row can incur two protection <page faults>, one for each writer, while making only one copy. A handler that always allocates on a protected write can make a second copy and free the now-unreferenced original: allocation counts and final resident storage are different observables.

These are model counts, not measured Linux costs or a guaranteed bound on total <computer memory>. More descendants, other owners, <demand paging>, <page replacement>, different <memory page> sizes, and different exclusive-ownership rules change the accounting. The useful claim is conditional: deferred data copying can cost in proportion to the distinct shared <memory pages> that are written, while creating separate <page tables> has its own cost.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Thrashing
{parent=Virtual memory}
{wiki}
{disambiguate=computer science}

= Memory thrashing
{synonym}

<Memory thrashing> is repeated costly cycling of <memory pages> out of and back into <computer memory>, leaving little useful computation between <page faults>. Active demand can exceed the available <page frames>, especially when <multiprogramming> admits too many <operating-system processes>. A high <page fault rate> during initial loading alone does not establish <memory thrashing>. <Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Beyond Physical Memory: Policies>[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 22, section 22.11: Thrashing]{ref}

Consider one <operating-system process> making the 20 <memory references> $1,2,3,4,1,2,3,4,\ldots,1,2,3,4$: five repetitions of four <memory pages>. Start with an empty <resident set>, use <demand paging> and exact <LRU replacement>, and assume no prefetching or competing <operating-system processes>. A hit finds the requested <memory page> resident; a miss requires a nonresident <page fault>. With three <page frames>, references 1–3 load pages 1–3. Reference 4 evicts page 1, reference 5 reloads page 1 and evicts page 2, and the cycle continues: every return finds its <memory page> evicted. With four <page frames>, only the first four references miss; all four <memory pages> then remain resident.

|| Available <page frames>
|| Misses in all 20 <memory references>
|| Overall <page fault rate>
|| Misses in references 5–20
|| <Page fault rate> in references 5–20
| 3
| 20
| 100%
| 16
| 100%
| 4
| 4
| 20%
| 0
| 0%

The four initial misses with four <page frames> are compulsory first-use misses from the empty <resident set>. They are startup work; the three-<page frame> case keeps faulting after startup. This makes the difference between <memory thrashing> and loading a new <working set> visible. For the three-<page frame> trace, each reused <memory page> has three distinct intervening <memory pages>, so <LRU replacement> has already evicted it before its next use.

\Image[memory-thrashing-lru-capacity.png]
{title=The same 20 memory references under <LRU replacement>: three <page frames> miss every time, four miss only during startup}

To quantify the cost, assume each hit takes 100 nanoseconds and each miss incurs an additional 1 millisecond of <page fault> service, followed by the same 100-nanosecond memory access. These are toy latencies, not measurements. With miss <probability> $p$, the <expected value> of access time is
$$
E[T]=100\,\mathrm{ns}+p\,(1\,\mathrm{ms}).
$$
The service cost is additional: it does not include the final 100 nanoseconds. Thus $p=1$ gives $1{,}000{,}100\,\mathrm{ns}$ and $p=0.2$ gives $200{,}100\,\mathrm{ns}$, approximately a fivefold ratio over the complete trace. Over references 5–20, the corresponding ratio is $1{,}000{,}100/100=10{,}001$: repeated <page faults> dominate the useful accesses. The <expected value> model follows the additive miss-penalty convention of OSTEP chapter 22, section 22.1. <Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Beyond Physical Memory: Policies>[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 22, equation 22.1: average memory access time]{ref}

Not every <page fault> performs disk <input-output>: <demand paging> can allocate zero-filled <memory pages>, and <copy-on-write> can copy an existing <page frame>. The toy service cost models expensive misses; the miss trace alone establishes residency behavior, while <memory thrashing> additionally concerns repeated costly cycling. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/vm-complete.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 23, section 23.1, Other Neat Tricks: demand zeroing and copy-on-write]{ref}

Possible remedies are more resident <page frames>, less <multiprogramming>, or reorganizing accesses to improve <temporal locality>. For this toy <sequence>, one extra <page frame> suffices under <LRU replacement>; that is not a universal threshold or policy. <Page replacement> alone cannot make every simultaneously needed <memory page> fit.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Working set
{parent=Virtual memory}
{wiki}
{title2=$W(t,\Delta)$}

An <operating-system process>'s <working set> is the <set> of distinct <memory pages> used in a declared recent window. Here the window counts <memory references>, not clock time: let $r_i$ identify the <memory page> accessed by the $i$th <memory reference>, and let the positive <integer> $\Delta$ be the window length. Immediately after reference $t$,
$$
W(t,\Delta)=\{r_i:\max(1,t-\Delta+1)\leq i\leq t\},\qquad W(0,\Delta)=\varnothing.
$$
Repeated <memory references> to the same <memory page> contribute only one <set element>. The <working set size> is the <cardinality> $|W(t,\Delta)|$. This recent footprint estimates current demand; it cannot guarantee which <memory pages> future <memory references> will need. The <resident set> instead records which <memory pages> are actually present in <computer memory>.

For the 20-reference <sequence> in <memory thrashing> and $\Delta=4$, the successive <working sets> begin $\{1\}$, $\{1,2\}$, $\{1,2,3\}$ and $\{1,2,3,4\}$. At $t=5$, the window is references 2–5, namely $2,3,4,1$, so $W(5,4)=\{1,2,3,4\}$ again. Every later four-reference window contains all four <memory pages>. Under three-<page frame> <LRU replacement>, however, the <resident set> at $t=5$ is $\{1,3,4\}$: page 2 was evicted. Consequently the <working set size> is four even though only three <memory pages> are resident. Under four-<page frame> <LRU replacement>, the <resident set> contains the complete <working set> after reference 4.

\Image[working-set-reference-window.png]
{title=A four-reference <working set> remains four pages while a three-frame <resident set> contains only three}

Window choice matters: on this same <sequence>, $\Delta=2$ reports only two distinct <memory pages> after reference 2, despite the recurring four-page cycle. A short window forgets useful <memory pages> sooner; a long window retains evidence from older phases. Enlarging the window can only enlarge or preserve the <working set> at fixed $t$, because the shorter window is contained in the longer one. No single window is best for every workload.

For a concrete <multiprogramming> budget, suppose three <operating-system processes> each have a four-page <working set>, no shared <page frames>, and only eight <page frames> are available for their data. Their combined demand is 12 <page frames>; all three <working sets> cannot be resident together. Letting two <operating-system processes> run while the third waits makes their eight-page demand fit in this simplified model. Real budgets also reserve <computer memory> for the <operating system> and other uses; shared <memory pages> must not be counted repeatedly as different physical copies.

Peter J. Denning's https://denninginstitute.com/pjd/PUBS/WSModel_1968.pdf[The Working Set Model for Program Behavior, Communications of the ACM, May 1968, sections 3–4]{ref} formalized the <working set> using recent process execution time, rather than this article's reference count. Its central insight connected memory demand to <process scheduling>: deciding which <operating-system processes> run also decides which <working sets> need room. <Temporal locality> makes recent use informative, without making it a promise about the future. The beautiful distinction is between a large possible <address space> and the smaller footprint of the current activity.

= Working set size
{parent=Working set}
{title2=$|W(t,\Delta)|$}

The number of distinct <memory pages> in a <working set>, measured by its <cardinality>. It depends on both the current <memory reference> index $t$ and the declared window $\Delta$, not just on the <resident set> capacity.

= Resident set
{parent=Virtual memory}

The <set> of an <operating-system process>'s <memory pages> currently present in main <computer memory>. A <resident set> may omit recently used <memory pages> after <page replacement>, or retain <memory pages> that have left the chosen <working set> window.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Memory-mapped file
{parent=Virtual memory}
{wiki}

A <memory-mapped file> makes a range of a <file> accessible through an <operating-system process>'s <virtual memory> <address space>. Once the mapping exists, ordinary loads and stores address its contents instead of explicitly reading them into a separate application buffer. This is attractive for jumping between records in a large <file>: an offset becomes an address calculation, and <demand paging> can supply the needed <memory pages>. It removes the application's explicit read-and-copy step; it does not promise that every access avoids copying, <input-output>, <page faults> or overhead, or that mapping is universally faster.

The <backing file>, <page cache> contents and each <operating-system process>'s <page table> play different roles. For ordinary buffered <Linux kernel> mappings, the <page cache> holds reusable <file> contents; normal reads and writes also use it. A valid mapping can exist before this <operating-system process> has a usable <page table entry>. A first load can therefore trigger a <page fault> even when another reader has already brought the contents into <computer memory>. If the contents are absent, servicing the <page fault> can require <input-output>; if present, the handler can install an <address translation> without that read. Special paths that bypass the <page cache> are outside this explanation.<Page Cache by The kernel development community>[Page Cache, opening paragraph: normal reads, writes and mmaps go through the page cache; bypass paths exist]{ref}

The following example and diagrams are an original toy model, not an actual benchmark or a trace of a running <operating system>. They assume ordinary buffered <file> mappings, successful accesses, two cooperating <operating-system processes>, no concurrent resizing, and explicitly ordered actions. <Memory-mapped file offset translation> explains where the accesses land; <shared file mappings> and <private file mappings> explain whose contents change; <memory-mapped file synchronization> explains the separate persistence boundary.

= Backing file
{parent=Memory-mapped file}

The <file> supplying a <memory-mapped file>'s initial contents. Its logical <file offsets> identify data independently of the <virtual addresses> chosen by different <operating-system processes>. Modified <private file mapping> contents have private storage; they are not updates waiting to be written to the <backing file>.

= mmap
{parent=Memory-mapped file}
{c}

The <POSIX> mapping interface. Ordinary <Linux kernel> <file> mappings require a positive length and an aligned <file offset>. A null address lets the <operating-system kernel> choose an aligned base; length need not be aligned. Check for `MAP_FAILED`. After success the <file descriptor> may be closed without invalidating the mapping; <munmap> or process termination removes it.<mmap(2) — Linux manual page>[DESCRIPTION: address, length, offset and descriptor lifetime; munmap; RETURN VALUE]{ref}

Mapping does not extend the <backing file>. Under <POSIX>, for an initially partial last <memory page>, the tail beyond the <file>'s end is zero-filled and is not written back as <file> data; accesses to whole mapped <memory pages> beyond the end raise `SIGBUS`. <POSIX> leaves access to portions added or removed by later resizing unspecified. Establish the required <file> size first and coordinate resizing with readers; a mapping's length is not proof that those <bytes> exist in the <file>.<The Open Group Base Specifications Issue 8, IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 by IEEE and others/mmap — POSIX.1-2024>[POSIX.1-2024, DESCRIPTION: partial end page, whole pages beyond the object, and changed file size; RATIONALE: references beyond the end do not extend the object]{ref}

The <Linux kernel> documents a tail-cache exception: modified beyond-end <bytes> can survive in the <page cache> and appear in later mappings, although never written to the <file>. Do not use that tail as reliable storage.<mmap(2) — Linux manual page>[BUGS: modified contents beyond the object end can remain in the page cache]{ref}

= munmap
{parent=mmap}
{c}

Removes a mapping from an <operating-system process>'s <address space>. Mapping lifetime is separate from <file descriptor> lifetime; removal also does not supply the completed writeback promised by <memory-mapped file synchronization>.

= Memory-mapped file offset translation
{parent=Memory-mapped file}
{title2=$o=O+(v-B)$}

Let $P$ be the <memory page> size, $O$ the aligned starting <file offset>, $L$ the mapping length and $B$ its base <virtual address>. A valid displacement $r=v-B$ with $0\leq r<L$ corresponds to <file offset>
$$
o=O+r,\qquad j=\left\lfloor\frac{r}{P}\right\rfloor,\qquad d=r\bmod P.
$$
Here $j$ is the relative <memory page> index, the <floor function> selects its <integer> part, and <Euclidean division> gives the within-page displacement $d$. The backing page begins at $O+jP$. This identifies <file> contents; the <page table> separately selects a resident <page frame> and enforces permissions.

Take $P=4096$, $O=4096$ and $L=8192$ <bytes> in a <file> of at least 12288 <bytes>. A has a <shared file mapping> at $B_A=\mathtt{0x10000000}$. B has a <shared file mapping> at $B_B=\mathtt{0x20000000}$ and a <private file mapping> at $B_C=\mathtt{0x30000000}$. These bases are illustrative chosen results, not requested fixed addresses. All three mappings cover the same two backing pages: <file offsets> 4096–8191 and 8192–12287. Displacement $r=4133=4096+37$ selects relative page 1, within-page displacement 37 and <file offset> $o=8229=\mathtt{0x2025}$. Thus A's `0x10001025`, B's shared `0x20001025` and B's private `0x30001025` initially identify the same <byte>. The adjacent displacement 4134 identifies <file offset> 8230. Different <virtual addresses> can reach the same backing contents.

\Image[memory-mapped-file-offset-page-mapping.png]
{title=Two <operating-system processes> map two backing pages at different <virtual addresses>; displacement 4133 identifies <file offset> 8229}

To access a requested <file offset> $q$ that is not aligned, choose $O=P\lfloor q/P\rfloor$ and displacement $q-O$. Mapping enough <bytes> to cover that displacement plus the requested length makes the desired data start inside the aligned mapping. Do not pass the unaligned $q$ directly as the <Linux kernel> `mmap` offset. For example, $q=8229$ uses $O=8192$ and displacement 37; that one-page start differs from our two-page mapping's $O=4096$.

= Shared file mapping
{parent=Memory-mapped file}
{title2=MAP_SHARED}

With `MAP_SHARED`, updates are visible to other <operating-system processes> mapping the same region and are carried through to the <backing file>. With `MAP_PRIVATE`, updates instead stay private, and later <backing file> changes have unspecified visibility in that mapping.<mmap(2) — Linux manual page>[DESCRIPTION, The flags argument: MAP_SHARED and MAP_PRIVATE]{ref}

In the toy model, both <shared file mappings> initially read backing <bytes> $x=7,y=8$ at <file offsets> 8229 and 8230. Both <memory pages> have been accessed, so their translations are installed. A shared store changes the common <page cache> contents and creates a <dirty memory page> eligible for <page writeback>. That change can be observed before it reaches persistent storage. Cooperating readers need <synchronization (computing)> to know when an update is ready; `MAP_SHARED` alone supplies no <mutual exclusion> or indivisible multi-field update.

= Private file mapping
{parent=Memory-mapped file}
{title2=MAP_PRIVATE}

With `MAP_PRIVATE`, <copy-on-write> initially reuses backing <page cache> contents. In our model, a private store raises a protection <page fault>, copies the affected <memory page> to a private <page frame>, installs a writable <page table entry> and retries. The other <memory page> can remain shared. This <page fault> needs no storage read.

In B's <private file mapping>, storing $x=99$ copies relative page 1, including its neighbor $y=8$. Next, A stores $x=11,y=12$ through its <shared file mapping>. After coordination outside the mapped data, B's shared reads give $(11,12)$, while its detached private page gives $(99,8)$. The untouched first <memory page> remains backed by the <file>; this is not a frozen snapshot of the whole <file>. Neither <page writeback> of shared contents nor <msync> publishes B's private 99. To save those private contents, the application needs a separate explicit <file> update.

\Image[memory-mapped-file-sharing-private-write-timeline.png]
{title=Toy <shared file mapping> and <copy-on-write> timeline: visible shared changes precede the chosen durable completion; private changes remain separate}

= Memory-mapped file synchronization
{parent=Memory-mapped file}

There are two distinct questions: whether another <operating-system process> can observe shared contents, and whether those contents survive a crash. In the timeline, persistent storage deliberately remains $(7,8)$ while the shared <page cache> contains $(11,12)$. No background <page writeback> occurs in this toy interval. Real <page writeback> may persist some contents earlier; shared visibility alone cannot tell the reader which contents are durable.

For the covered shared range, `msync(base, length, MS_SYNC)` requests <page writeback> and waits for completion; check its return value. `MS_ASYNC` is not a durable completion acknowledgment, and on modern <Linux kernel> versions it is a no-op because dirty contents are already tracked. The address must be aligned, so synchronizing our changed second page uses `base + 4096`, length 4096, rather than `base + 4133`. Unmapping alone is not a guarantee of completed writeback.<msync(2) — Linux manual page by Linux man-pages project>[DESCRIPTION, RETURN VALUE, ERRORS and VERSIONS: covered range, MS_SYNC versus MS_ASYNC, page-aligned address, and Linux MS_ASYNC behavior since 2.6.19]{ref}

For persistence of the <file> and associated <file metadata>, <fsync> on an open <file descriptor> is a broader interface; a new <directory entry> needs separate synchronization of its <file-system directory>. Retain a suitable <file descriptor> if the persistence protocol needs it after mapping. Successful storage completion still relies on the <file system> and device honoring their persistence contracts.<fsync(2) — Linux manual page by Linux man-pages project>[DESCRIPTION, RETURN VALUE and ERRORS: file data and metadata, directory entry, completion and reported failures]{ref}

Neither <fsync> nor <msync> supplies <mutual exclusion>, an <atomic storage write> for several <memory pages>, or an application-wide <file-system transaction>. Stop or coordinate writers before deciding which update a completion acknowledges. A crash during <page writeback> can leave a mixture of old and new contents unless a separate recovery protocol provides <file-system crash consistency>. The last timeline row assumes successful synchronous completion, honored <storage flush> semantics and no subsequent writer; it illustrates a persistence boundary, not a promise about arbitrary hardware failures.

= msync
{parent=Memory-mapped file synchronization}
{c}

A mapping-range synchronization interface used by <memory-mapped file synchronization>. Its `MS_SYNC` mode concerns completed updates to a <backing file>; it does not merge <private file mapping> changes into that <file> or coordinate competing readers and writers.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Memory segmentation
{parent=Virtual memory}
{wiki}

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Paging
{parent=Virtual memory}
{wiki}

<Paging> divides an <address space> into fixed-size <memory pages> and main <computer memory> into equally sized <page frames>. Here assume <byte> addressing, one <memory page> size $P=2^b$ <bytes>, and an unsigned <virtual address> $v$. Its virtual page number $q$ and offset $d$ are
$$
q=\left\lfloor\frac{v}{P}\right\rfloor,\qquad d=v\bmod P,\qquad 0\leq d<P.
$$
The brackets denote the <floor function>, and $\bmod$ gives the <remainder> in <Euclidean division>. If the <page table> maps $q$ to physical frame number $f$ and permits the access, the <physical address> is
$$
a=fP+d.
$$
The low $b$ <bits> are unchanged; the other <bits> select the <memory page> or <page frame>. A <bit shift> and <bit mask> can extract these fields. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/vm-paging.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 18: Paging, Introduction, sections 18.1–18.2]{ref}

For a 16-<bit> <virtual address> and $P=256=2^8$ <bytes>, take $v=\mathtt{0x1234}=4660$ in <hexadecimal>. Then $q=18=\mathtt{0x12}$ and $d=52=\mathtt{0x34}$. A mapping to $f=7=\mathtt{0x07}$ gives
$$
a=7\cdot256+52=1844=\mathtt{0x0734}.
$$
Thus the eight high <bits> choose a mapping; the eight low <bits> choose the same position in the resulting <page frame>. The 256-<byte> size is for illustration, not a claim about an actual architecture's supported <memory page> sizes.

\Image[virtual-memory-address-translation.png]
{title=Translating 0x1234 through an illustrative page table, with a separate offset and access check}

The useful freedom is that adjacent <memory pages> can occupy unrelated <page frames>. In the diagram, virtual pages `0x12` and `0x13` occupy <page frames> `0x07` and `0x02`; <virtual addresses> `0x12ff` and `0x1300` are consecutive, but translate to <physical addresses> `0x07ff` and `0x0200`. Uniform units simplify placement and <page replacement>, at the cost of <page table> storage and unused <bytes> within partly filled <memory pages>. For example, placing 300 <bytes> in exclusive 256-<byte> <page frames> requires two <page frames>, leaving $512-300=212$ <bytes> unused.

= Memory page
{parent=Paging}
{wiki=Page_(computer_memory)}

A contiguous unit of an <address space> used for <paging>. In the example, virtual page `0x12` consists of <virtual addresses> `0x1200` through `0x12ff`.

= Page frame
{parent=Paging}

A slot in main <computer memory> that can hold one <memory page> of the selected size. Physical frame number `0x07` with 256-<byte> <page frames> starts at <physical address> `0x0700`.

= Demand paging
{parent=Paging}
{wiki}

Making a <memory page> resident when it is first accessed, using a <page fault> to request the work. It can involve loading stored contents or allocating newly initialized contents.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Page replacement
{parent=Paging}

Choosing a resident <memory page> to evict when another <memory page> needs an occupied <page frame>. An unused <page frame> needs no eviction. A <dirty memory page> requires preservation before reuse, accounted for separately as <page writeback>. A <page replacement> policy balances likely future reuse against the overhead of tracking past <memory references>.

The opportunity comes from <temporal locality>: retaining a recently accessed <memory page> can spare a later nonresident <page fault>. <Spatial locality> helps consecutive accesses reuse the same <memory page> until they cross its boundary. A <working set> describes recent demand, while the <resident set> describes what is actually present. A small active footprint can fit in the available <page frames> even when the whole <address space> cannot. These are useful workload tendencies, not guarantees about future <memory references>.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Beyond Physical Memory: Policies>[Section 22.5, pp. 7–8, and the Types of Locality aside on p. 8: recent use motivates retention, but locality is a heuristic]{ref}

The <page-reference trace> below uses the counterexample discussed in <Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others> (chapter 22, p. 5) and makes the decisions explicit. <FIFO page replacement> ranks arrival time, <LRU replacement> ranks last access, <CLOCK page replacement> samples recent use, and <optimal page replacement> knows the remaining <sequence> in advance. Count misses first; <page-replacement cost accounting> separately counts <page writeback> and explains why fewer misses need not mean less total service time.

// Destination: computer-science.bigb

= CLOCK page replacement
{parent=Page replacement}
{c}

Keep <page frames> in a circular list with a reference <bit> and a scanning hand. Clear set reference <bits> while scanning; choose the first eligible <page frame> with a clear reference <bit>. This second chance approximates <LRU replacement> with cheaper tracking. When hardware reference flags are absent, revoking a mapping and handling its first later <page fault> can supply a software reference observation.

For the <page-reference trace>, number the three <page frames> 1–3 and initially point the hand at 1. Every access sets the referenced <page frame>'s reference <bit> to 1. On a miss, scan from the hand: stop at an empty <page frame> or a resident one with reference <bit> 0; otherwise clear its reference <bit> and advance cyclically. Install the requested <memory page> with reference <bit> 1 and advance the hand to the next <page frame>. Hits leave the hand where it is. This fixes a particular variant; clean-victim preference is excluded.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Beyond Physical Memory: Policies>[Section 22.8, p. 12: reference bits and the circular-hand algorithm; section 22.9, p. 13: dirty-page preference is an additional modification]{ref}

After the first three accesses, all reference <bits> are 1 and the hand is back at <page frame> 1. Access 4 clears all three reference <bits>, returns to <page frame> 1, evicts <memory page> 1, installs <memory page> 4, and leaves the hand at <page frame> 2 with reference <bits> $(1,0,0)$. This variant gives nine misses for three <page frames> and ten for four on the displayed <sequence>. Approximating <LRU replacement> therefore does not inherit the <LRU stack property>.

// Destination: computer-science.bigb

= LRU replacement
{parent=Page replacement}
{c}

= Least recently used replacement
{synonym}

Evict the least recently accessed eligible item. Exact recency tracking on every <memory reference> can be expensive, whereas accesses to a <buffer cache> already pass through software. Recency captures <temporal locality> but a long scan can evict frequently reused items before their next use.

For <page replacement>, update recency on hits as well as misses; evict only when a miss requires a <page frame> and all eligible <page frames> are occupied. The four-page cycle in <memory thrashing> shows how exact <LRU replacement> behaves at capacities three and four.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Beyond Physical Memory: Policies>[Chapter 22, section 22.5, pp. 7–8: least-recently-used eviction; section 22.7, p. 11: update recency on every access]{ref}

The <LRU stack property> compares different capacities on one fixed <page-reference trace>; it does not say that <LRU replacement> always beats <FIFO page replacement>. On the displayed <sequence>, three-<page frame> <LRU replacement> has ten misses, versus nine for <FIFO page replacement>, while four-<page frame> <LRU replacement> has only eight.

= LRU stack property
{parent=LRU replacement}
{c}
{title2=$R_k(t)\subseteq R_{k+1}(t)$}

For exact <LRU replacement>, let $R_k(t)$ be the <resident set> after the first $t$ accesses of a fixed <page-reference trace> with capacity $k$. With empty starts, identical access order, freely placeable <memory pages>, and all resident <memory pages> eligible for eviction,
$$
R_k(t)\subseteq R_{k+1}(t)\qquad\text{for every }t\geq0.
$$
Here $\subseteq$ means <subset>. At every time, form one capacity-independent ordered list of distinct <memory pages>, most recently accessed first. An access moves its <memory page> to the front, preserving the order of the others. Exact <LRU replacement> with $k$ <page frames> retains the first $k$ entries, or all entries if fewer than $k$ have appeared. The first $k$ entries are contained in the first $k+1$, proving the property. This recency list is not the push/pop interface of a <LIFO stack>.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Beyond Physical Memory: Policies>[Belady's Anomaly aside, p. 5: stack-property inclusion and its consequence for LRU; section 22.7, p. 11: exact recency must be updated on every access]{ref}

Apply inclusion immediately before an access: a hit with $k$ <page frames> must also hit with $k+1$. Thus cumulative misses with $k+1$ cannot exceed those with $k$, at any prefix of the same <page-reference trace>. On the displayed <sequence>, the counts decrease from ten to eight when capacity increases from three to four. Arbitrary initial contents, different access orders, protected victims, or clean-victim preference fall outside the proof. Nor does it prove monotonic <page writeback> costs, or apply automatically to <CLOCK page replacement> just because that policy approximates <LRU replacement>.

// Destination: computer-science.bigb

= FIFO page replacement
{parent=Page replacement}
{c}

Evict the <memory page> loaded earliest among currently resident <memory pages>, without refreshing its position on access. It is simple but ignores <temporal locality> and can evict a frequently accessed <memory page>.

Implement this ranking with a <FIFO queue> of resident <memory pages>: append on loading, remove the oldest on eviction, and leave the queue unchanged on a hit. <Belady anomaly> shows why preserving arrival order can give more misses with more <page frames>. The comparison uses the same <page-reference trace> and empty initial <resident sets> in both runs.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Beyond Physical Memory: Policies>[Section 22.3, pp. 4–5: FIFO ordering; Belady's Anomaly aside, p. 5: the twelve-reference counterexample and contrast with stack policies]{ref}

= Page-reference trace
{parent=Page replacement}
{title2=$(r_1,\ldots,r_n)$}

A <memory access trace> reduced to the ordered <sequence> of accessed <memory page> identities, retaining repetitions. An address-only reduction loses read/write information, so annotate writes when studying <dirty memory pages>. Use the following classic <Belady anomaly> <sequence>, where the numbers label <memory pages>, not <physical addresses>:
$$
1,2,3,4,1,2,5,1,2,3,4,5.
$$
Start with three empty, equal-sized <page frames> allocated to one <operating-system process>. Every <memory page> can occupy any <page frame>, and every resident <memory page> is eligible for eviction. Use <demand paging>: a hit finds the requested <memory page> in the <resident set>; a miss causes exactly one nonresident <page fault> and loads it before the next access. Fill the lowest-numbered empty <page frame>, then reuse the victim's <page frame>. Assume only reads, no fetching ahead of requests, no competing <operating-system processes>, and no external removal of <memory pages>. <CLOCK page replacement> uses its declared hand rule, which fills the same initial <page frames> here. The <memory page> labels alone do not determine the result without these assumptions.

\Image[page-replacement-three-frame-trace.png]
{title=All twelve accesses with three <page frames>: <FIFO page replacement>, exact <LRU replacement>, offline <optimal page replacement> and the stated <CLOCK page replacement> variant}

Each column shows the state after servicing that access. Rows keep physical <page frame> positions fixed, rather than silently sorting the <resident set>. The <CLOCK page replacement> entries also show reference <bits> and the next hand position. The independently computed totals are:

\Table[
\Tr[\Th[Policy]\Th[Miss indices, counted from 1]\Th[Misses]\Th[Hits]]
\Tr[\Td[<FIFO page replacement>]\Td[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10, 11]\Td[9]\Td[3]]
\Tr[\Td[<LRU replacement>]\Td[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10, 11, 12]\Td[10]\Td[2]]
\Tr[\Td[<Optimal page replacement>]\Td[1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 10, 11]\Td[7]\Td[5]]
\Tr[\Td[<CLOCK page replacement>]\Td[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10, 11]\Td[9]\Td[3]]
]

The first three misses fill unused <page frames>; the fourth miss needs eviction. At access 4, <FIFO page replacement> and <LRU replacement> evict <memory page> 1. <Optimal page replacement> instead evicts <memory page> 3: the next uses of resident <memory pages> 1, 2 and 3 are at indices 5, 6 and 10. At access 10, <FIFO page replacement> evicts <memory page> 1 while <LRU replacement> evicts <memory page> 5. Consequently the last access to <memory page> 5 hits under <FIFO page replacement> but misses under <LRU replacement>. Matching totals for <FIFO page replacement> and <CLOCK page replacement> on this <sequence> do not make the policies equivalent. The generators compute every displayed state; a separate search over all legal victims confirms the seven-miss <optimal page replacement> bound for three <page frames>.

= Optimal page replacement
{parent=Page replacement}

On a miss with all <page frames> occupied, evict the resident <memory page> whose next access is farthest in the remaining <page-reference trace>. Treat no further access as farther than any finite next-use index; in the diagram, ties choose the lowest-numbered <page frame>. This is an offline, clairvoyant benchmark: a general <operating system> cannot know its future <memory references>. It minimizes miss count in the stated fixed-capacity <demand paging> model, rather than predicting the future from <temporal locality>.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Beyond Physical Memory: Policies>[Section 22.2, pp. 2–4: farthest-future MIN policy, minimum misses, and the unavailable-future limitation]{ref}

In the <page-reference trace>, seven misses with three <page frames> is therefore a lower bound for any policy using the same assumptions; with four <page frames> the corresponding minimum is six. The benchmark is useful for judging how much improvement is possible on a recorded workload. Its objective counts each miss equally. Different <page writeback> costs or unequal loading costs require a different objective; minimum misses alone does not prove minimum elapsed time.

= Bélády's anomaly
{parent=Page replacement}
{c}
{wiki}

= Belady anomaly
{c}
{synonym}

Increasing the number of <page frames> can increase misses under a given <page replacement> policy on the same <page-reference trace>. For <FIFO page replacement> on the displayed twelve-reference <sequence>, an empty start with three <page frames> gives nine misses, while an empty start with four gives ten. The larger run hits only at indices 5 and 6; it misses at 1–4 and 7–12. These are miss counts, not observed running times.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Beyond Physical Memory: Policies>[Belady's Anomaly aside, p. 5: the reference sequence and increasing capacity from three to four]{ref}

\Image[fifo-belady-anomaly-frame-comparison.png]
{title=<FIFO page replacement> queue states and cumulative misses: four <page frames> give ten misses versus nine with three}

The explanation is visible before the final count. After access 6, the three-<page frame> queue is $(4,1,2)$ and the four-<page frame> queue is $(1,2,3,4)$, both oldest first. Loading <memory page> 5 at access 7 removes <memory page> 4 in the smaller run but <memory page> 1 in the larger. Their resulting <resident sets> are $\{1,2,5\}$ and $\{2,3,4,5\}$. The smaller <resident set> is not a <subset> of the larger: its next access to <memory page> 1 hits while the larger run misses. Different earlier evictions create different histories; an extra <page frame> is not simply an extra unchanged slot. <FIFO page replacement> can improve on other <sequences>; the anomaly is an existence claim, not a claim that more <page frames> usually hurt.

= Dirty memory page
{parent=Page replacement}

A resident <memory page> with modifications that must be preserved in its backing storage before its <page frame> can be reused. In the simple model here, a write sets its dirty flag, and successful <page writeback> clears it. A clean <memory page> with a recoverable backing copy can be discarded without an outgoing data write.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Beyond Physical Memory: Policies>[Section 22.9, p. 13: modified/dirty bit and the outgoing I/O difference between dirty and clean eviction]{ref}

= Page writeback
{parent=Dirty memory page}

Preserving the modified contents of a <dirty memory page> in its backing storage. The reference model waits for successful <page writeback> before reusing the victim's <page frame>; failure cannot authorize losing its modifications. A reference <bit> records recent access, while the dirty flag records a write obligation: reading can set the first without setting the second. <Buffer cache writeback> concerns the analogous obligation for a <buffered device block>.

= Page-replacement cost accounting
{parent=Page replacement}
{title2=$T=nh+Mr+Dw$}

Let $n$ be the number of accesses, $M$ the miss count and $D$ the number of dirty-victim <page writebacks>. Assume every requested <memory page> has a backing copy, every miss reads it, writes modify only part of its contents, transfers succeed and occur serially, and there is no background or final flushing. With common access cost $h$, additional load cost $r$ per miss, and additional outgoing write cost $w$ per dirty victim, an illustrative total service time is
$$
T=nh+Mr+Dw.
$$
Bookkeeping and other <operating system> costs are omitted. The two transfer directions are separate: a miss can require a read with no write, or both a victim write and a requested-page read. A retained <dirty memory page> contributes no outgoing write to this finite-<page-reference trace> count until evicted. Future flushing would need its own accounting boundary.

For three-<page frame> <FIFO page replacement> on the displayed <page-reference trace>, all reads give $(n,M,D)=(12,9,0)$. Change only access 1 to a partial write of <memory page> 1. Its initial miss still loads the old contents, the write marks it dirty, and eviction at access 4 adds exactly one <page writeback>. Later loads of <memory page> 1 are clean because the remaining accesses read. The result is $(12,9,1)$: identical residency and nine misses, but one extra outgoing write. With illustrative $h=100$ nanoseconds, $r=1$ millisecond and $w=2$ milliseconds, total service time changes from $9.0012$ to $11.0012$ milliseconds. These are model costs, not hardware measurements.

Preferring a clean victim can save an immediate <page writeback>, but evicting a soon-needed clean <memory page> can add later misses. This tradeoff is why a miss-minimizing <optimal page replacement> benchmark and a minimum-cost policy are different questions. Real <page faults> can also initialize a new <memory page> or perform <copy-on-write> without reading backing storage; those cases require different load costs.

// Destination: computer-science.bigb

= Page fault
{parent=Paging}
{wiki}

A <page fault> transfers control to the <operating system> when an access cannot proceed under the current <paging> translation or protection rules. The term includes both unavailable translations and forbidden accesses on <x86>. Intel's manual distinguishes these causes; AMD's error code likewise distinguishes ordinary not-present faults from protection violations. Other architectural causes, including malformed <page table entries>, also exist. https://cdrdv2-public.intel.com/671200/325462-sdm-vol-1-2abcd-3abcd.pdf[Intel SDM, volume 3A, section 4.7: Page-Fault Exceptions]{ref}https://docs.amd.com/api/khub/documents/sD1_QL~h4Afq2_tvzxqqSQ/content[AMD64 Architecture Programmer's Manual, volume 2, revision 3.44, section 8.4.2: Page-Fault Error Code]{ref}

The illustrative <page table> in <paging> separates three situations:
* A read at <virtual address> `0x1434` targets a permitted but nonresident <memory page>. The <operating system> may supply it and retry the access.
* A write at <virtual address> `0x1234` targets a resident, read-only <memory page>. The <page fault> is caused by missing write permission, not by missing contents.
* A read at <virtual address> `0x1534` targets an unmapped part of the <address space>. The <operating system> must reject it unless its policy authorizes creating a mapping.

A <page fault> is not necessarily a program error or a storage-device read. <Demand paging> can allocate a zero-filled <page frame> without reading secondary storage. A <copy-on-write> write deliberately encounters read-only permissions; the <operating system> supplies a private copy and enables the write. A truly forbidden access may instead terminate the <operating-system process>. The handler interprets the fault using both architectural fault information and its own mapping policy. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/vm-complete.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 23: demand zeroing and copy-on-write]{ref}

For a nonresident <memory page> whose contents must be loaded, the handler obtains a <page frame>, possibly invokes <page replacement>, supplies the contents, updates the <page table>, arranges any necessary <translation lookaside buffer> invalidation, and resumes the operation. If no mapping or permission can legally be supplied, it reports failure. This mechanism lets ordinary accesses request work only when it becomes necessary. <Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Beyond Physical Memory: Mechanisms>[Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others; Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 21: Beyond Physical Memory, Mechanisms, sections 21.3–21.5]{ref}

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Page fault rate
{parent=Page fault}
{title2=$p$}

For a specified interval of <memory references>, the number causing a <page fault> divided by the total number of <memory references>. In the nonresident-only toy model in <memory thrashing>, this equals the miss fraction used as <probability> $p$ in the <expected value> calculation. Startup and later intervals can have very different <page fault rates>.

= Translation lookaside buffer
{parent=Paging}
{wiki}
{title2=TLB}

A <translation lookaside buffer> is a small hardware <memory cache> of <virtual address> to <physical address> mappings and their access attributes. A <TLB hit> finds a usable cached mapping for the current <address space>; access permissions still apply. A <TLB miss> means that mapping is absent from the <translation lookaside buffer>. It says nothing by itself about whether the <memory page> is resident. Evicting a <translation lookaside buffer> entry removes a cached translation, not the <memory page>'s contents. https://docs.amd.com/api/khub/documents/sD1_QL~h4Afq2_tvzxqqSQ/content[AMD64 Architecture Programmer's Manual, volume 2, revision 3.44, section 5.5: Translation-Lookaside Buffer]{ref}

For the 256-<byte> example, a cached virtual page `0x12` to physical frame `0x07` mapping covers every offset from `0x00` to `0xff`. Reads at <virtual addresses> `0x1234` and `0x1235` can reuse it to reach <physical addresses> `0x0734` and `0x0735`. If the <translation lookaside buffer> is initially empty, the first read instead requires a <page table walk>; with a resident, readable mapping, it completes without a <page fault>. A write can fault even on a <TLB hit> because cached permissions deny it.

\Image[tlb-miss-versus-page-fault.png]
{title=A TLB miss can refill a resident translation without a page fault; permission failures can fault on a TLB hit}

The diagrams show an ordinary <x86> model with hardware <page table walks>. Some architectures use an <operating system> handler to refill the <translation lookaside buffer> after a <TLB miss>; that refill event alone is not a nonresident <page fault>. <Temporal locality> helps a small <translation lookaside buffer> serve repeated accesses, and one entry can cover many <bytes>. <Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Paging: Faster Translations (TLBs)>[Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others; Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 19: Paging, Faster Translations, sections 19.1 and 19.3]{ref}

A <context switch> does not universally flush the whole <translation lookaside buffer>. An <address space identifier> can distinguish translations with the same virtual page number in different <address spaces>; Intel's PCIDs support retaining translations across appropriate switches. The <operating system> must still invalidate stale translations when mappings or permissions change, following the architecture's rules, including coordination with other processors that could use them. https://cdrdv2-public.intel.com/825743/325462-sdm-vol-1-2abcd-3abcd-4.pdf[Intel SDM, volume 3A, sections 4.10.1 and 4.10.4–4.10.5: PCIDs and translation invalidation]{ref}

= TLB hit
{parent=Translation lookaside buffer}
{c}

A lookup that finds a valid cached <address translation> for the relevant <address space>. The access can still cause a <page fault> if the cached permissions forbid it.

For the 64-<byte> toy model in <address translation>, a cached process A page-4 mapping covers reads at both `0x12D` and `0x12E`, yielding <physical addresses> `0x36D` and `0x36E`. The <TLB hit> reuses the mapping; it does not guarantee that either requested <byte> is in a data <memory cache>.

= TLB miss
{parent=Translation lookaside buffer}
{c}

A lookup that fails to find the required cached <address translation>. A successful refill can leave the <memory page> resident throughout and cause no <page fault>.

For process A's resident virtual page 4 in <address translation>, discarding only its <translation lookaside buffer> entry makes the next read a <TLB miss> while its contents remain in <page frame> 13. <Page replacement> would instead remove the resident <memory page>; these operations invalidate different kinds of cached information.

= Address space identifier
{parent=Translation lookaside buffer}
{wiki}
{title2=ASID}

A tag distinguishing cached <address translations> belonging to different <address spaces>. Tags can be reused, but reusing a tag for a different mapping context requires appropriate invalidation.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Page table
{parent=Paging}
{wiki}

A <page table> is the <data structure> describing an <address space>'s mappings from virtual page numbers to <page frames>, together with access and status information. A <page table entry> can describe a resident <memory page> and its permissions. Nonresident or unmapped <memory pages> require a representation that lets the <operating system> distinguish them; a hardware not-present flag alone need not make that distinction. On <x86>, additional <operating system> information determines whether a not-present mapping is legitimate. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/vm-paging.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 18, section 18.3: page-table contents and the x86 present bit]{ref}

The four rows drawn under <paging> are an illustrative model, combining <page table> and <operating system> information: virtual page `0x12` is resident in physical frame `0x07` with read-only permission; `0x13` is resident in `0x02` with read/write permission; `0x14` is mapped read/write but nonresident; `0x15` is unmapped. The labels deliberately separate mapping policy, residency and permission. They are not a literal <x86> entry layout. A <translation lookaside buffer> valid flag is a fourth, independent notion: it says that a cached translation can be considered, not that every resident <memory page> has a cached translation.

A flat <array> with one <page table entry> per virtual page would have $2^{16}/256=256$ entries in this example. At an assumed four <bytes> per entry, it occupies $256\cdot4=1024$ <bytes>, whether one <memory page> or all 256 <memory pages> are mapped. A <multi-level page table> saves space in sparse <address spaces> by allocating lower-level tables only for the regions that need them. The tradeoff is extra dependent lookups during a <page table walk>; a <translation lookaside buffer> avoids repeating those lookups on a hit. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/vm-smalltables.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 20: Paging, Smaller Tables, section 20.3]{ref}

= Page table entry
{parent=Page table}
{title2=PTE}

One record in a <page table>, holding a <page frame> number or a next-level table reference together with architecture-dependent flags. Effective permissions in a <multi-level page table> can depend on several entries, not just the final one.

= Multi-level page table
{parent=Page table}

A hierarchy of <page tables> indexed by successive groups of virtual page number <bits>. Unused regions can omit lower-level tables.

The useful omission is an entire lower-level <array>, not merely an invalid <page table entry>. One absent upper-level entry can represent a large unmapped interval of the <address space>. The <Linux kernel>'s documented hierarchy uses this idea to avoid allocating tables for unused regions; its software levels can also be folded to match the hardware. <Page Tables by The kernel development community>[Page Tables: opening hierarchy discussion, paragraph beginning "One could of course imagine"; Page Table Folding]{ref}

= Two-level address translation
{parent=Multi-level page table}

Choose an original teaching model with 16-<bit> <virtual addresses>, 16-<bit> <physical addresses>, and 256-<byte> <memory pages> and <page frames>. The eight high <bits> identify a virtual <memory page>; divide these into two four-<bit> indices. The eight low <bits> are the offset within the <memory page>. Thus each <page table> has 16 entries: the root selects a lower-level <page table>, and that table selects a data <page frame>. Every <page table entry> is four <bytes>, including its reference and flags. These are chosen model parameters, not a hardware entry encoding.

For a <virtual address> $v$, extract the root index $i$, lower-level index $j$, and offset $d$ by <Euclidean division>:
$$
i=\left\lfloor\frac{v}{4096}\right\rfloor,\qquad
j=\left\lfloor\frac{v}{256}\right\rfloor\bmod16,\qquad
d=v\bmod256,\qquad
v=4096i+256j+d.
$$
Here $\lfloor\cdot\rfloor$ is the <floor function> and $\bmod$ denotes the remainder in <Euclidean division>. Each index ranges from 0 to 15, and $d$ ranges from 0 to 255. In <hexadecimal>, <virtual address> `0x1234` splits into $i=1$, $j=2$, and $d=\mathtt{0x34}=52$; its <binary numeral system> representation is `0001 | 0010 | 00110100`.

Fix the requested operation to be a one-<byte> user read. Assume both table nodes and the data <page frame> are resident, table reads are allowed, and no mapping changes during the <page table walk>. The model requires presence at both levels and takes the <set intersection> of the two entries' read, write, execute, and user-access permissions. This is an explicit <memory protection> rule for this model; <Sv39 page tables> use different rules. The root's <physical address> is `0x8000`; the relevant entries are:

\Table[
\Tr[\Th[Record]\Th[<Physical address> of record]\Th[Reference held in the <page table entry>]\Th[Presence and permissions]]
\Tr[\Td[Root entry 1]\Td[`0x8000 + 1*4 = 0x8004`]\Td[Lower-level table at `0x9000`]\Td[Present; user; read/write; no execute]]
\Tr[\Td[Lower-level entry 2]\Td[`0x9000 + 2*4 = 0x9008`]\Td[Data <page frame> `0x07`, base `0x0700`]\Td[Present; user; read-only; no execute]]
]

The first lookup obtains a table reference, not the final data <physical address>. The second lookup obtains <page frame> number 7. Both entries permit a user read, so the original offset is added to the data <page frame> base:
$$
a=7\cdot256+52=1844=\mathtt{0x0734}.
$$
The <page table walk> therefore reads records at `0x8004` and `0x9008`, then the requested read accesses data at `0x0734`. All three are distinct <physical addresses>. These are two dependent table lookups followed by one data access, not a claim of three accesses to main <computer memory>: a <memory cache> can satisfy table lookups, and cached intermediate translations can skip them.

\Image[two-level-page-table-address-walk.png]
{title=The model's 4+4+8-bit address split and complete read translation from 0x1234 to 0x0734}

Under the same <memory protection> rule, a user write at `0x1234` fails because lower-level entry 2 denies writes, even though root entry 1 allows them. Clearing either entry's presence flag also prevents the access. An absent root entry stops the <page table walk> before any lower-level lookup. A <page fault> then requires the <operating system> to determine whether a permitted mapping needs storage or the access is invalid; absence alone does not establish <demand paging> eligibility.

With an empty <translation lookaside buffer>, the permitted read causes a <TLB miss> and this successful <page table walk>, with no <page fault>. If the resulting mapping and permissions are cached for the current <address space>, a later read at `0x1235` can use a <TLB hit> to reach `0x0735`. A later write can cause a protection <page fault> on a <TLB hit> because the cached mapping remains read-only. Assume cached permissions are current; changing them requires the architecture's prescribed invalidation. A <TLB miss> describes a missing cached translation, while a <page fault> describes an access that cannot proceed under the translation or protection rules. <Page Tables by The kernel development community>[MMU, TLB, and Page Faults: translation caches, page walks, and faults for missing or forbidden mappings]{ref}

= Sparse page table allocation
{parent=Multi-level page table}

Use the <two-level address translation> model and map exactly four resident virtual <memory pages>, numbered `0x12`, `0x13`, `0xa0`, and `0xa1`. Assign their data <page frames> to `0x07`, `0x02`, `0x05`, and `0x06`, respectively. Only two root indices occur: 1 and 10. Allocate exactly three table nodes: the root at `0x8000`, the lower-level table for root index 1 at `0x9000`, and the lower-level table for root index 10 at `0xa000`. Root entries 1 and 10 reference those tables; all other root entries are absent. Lower-level entries 2 and 3 in the first table and entries 0 and 1 in the second table hold the four mappings; every other lower-level entry is absent. Use the read-only permissions already specified for virtual <memory page> `0x12`; let the other three mappings permit user reads and writes, with no execution, and let both populated root entries permit user reads and writes.

Each root index covers 16 virtual <memory pages>, hence $16\cdot256=4096$ <bytes> of the <address space>. A compact table node occupies $16\cdot4=64$ <bytes>. The three nodes therefore require $3\cdot64=192$ <bytes> of table storage, including the root and all absent entry slots in the allocated nodes. A single-level <array> requires $256\cdot4=1024$ <bytes> for the same <address space>, saving $1024-192=832$ <bytes> in the compact model. Four data <page frames> occupy another $4\cdot256=1024$ <bytes>; those contents are separate from table storage.

If instead the allocator dedicates a whole 256-<byte> <page frame> to every table node, the three nodes consume 768 <bytes>, leaving $3(256-64)=576$ <bytes> unused within those allocations. The single-level table fills four such <page frames> and still consumes 1024 <bytes>. Thus this example saves 256 <bytes> of allocated table storage under that alternative policy. Neither accounting includes allocator metadata, <operating-system process> records, or <translation lookaside buffer> storage; data allocations are the same in both designs.

\Image[sparse-page-table-node-footprint.png]
{title=All table nodes for four mapped pages, and the footprint change when the same four pages occupy four root regions}

Placement matters as well as mapping count. Four virtual <memory pages> `0x12`, `0x42`, `0x72`, and `0xa2` require the root plus four lower-level tables: five nodes, 320 compact <bytes>, or 1280 <bytes> with a dedicated <page frame> per node. Only four data <page frames> are still required. Let $g$ be the number of distinct root indices represented by the mappings. With no retained empty lower-level tables, table storage is $64(1+g)$ compact <bytes> or $256(1+g)$ allocated <bytes> under the dedicated-<page frame> policy. Even an empty <address space> retains the root in this accounting, so $g=0$ costs 64 or 256 <bytes>.

For a fully populated <address space>, $g=16$: 17 nodes cost 1088 compact <bytes> or 4352 allocated <bytes>. The single-level table remains 1024 <bytes>. Sparse allocation is therefore a conditional space benefit; scattered mappings, node allocation granularity, and the root's overhead can reverse it. Additional dependent lookups on a <TLB miss> also have a cost, but node counts alone establish no universal execution-time ordering.

= Sv39 page table
{parent=Multi-level page table}
{c}

The actual <Sv39 page table> geometry uses three nine-<bit> indices and a twelve-<bit> offset for base 4096-<byte> <memory pages>. Each table contains 512 eight-<byte> <page table entries>, exactly one 4096-<byte> <memory page>; the `satp` <processor register> identifies the root. A 64-<bit> <virtual address> must repeat bit 38 in bits 63 through 39. Higher-level terminal entries can also map aligned larger <memory pages>, shortening the <page table walk>. These rules differ from the 16-<bit>, four-<byte>-entry <two-level address translation> model. <The RISC-V Instruction Set Manual, Volume II: Privileged Architecture by RISC-V International>[Version 20260120, section 12.4.1, printed pp. 136–137, Figures 68–70: address fields, entry sizes, root, and larger pages]{ref}

For ordinary <Sv39 page tables>, valid nonterminal <page table entries> reference the next table with read, write, and execute flags clear; those clear flags do not prohibit reading the eventual data. The terminal entry supplies access permissions, with additional <processor privilege level> and status checks. An invalid entry, malformed encoding, or denied access can cause a <page fault>. Thus the model's <set intersection> of read/write permissions at both levels must not be applied mechanically to <Sv39 page tables>. <The RISC-V Instruction Set Manual, Volume II: Privileged Architecture by RISC-V International>[Version 20260120, section 12.3.2, printed p. 134, steps 2–8; section 12.4.1, printed p. 137: Sv39 uses the same algorithm with LEVELS=3 and PTESIZE=8]{ref}

In <xv6 RISC-V source code>, root allocation is separate from lower-level allocation: `uvmcreate()` allocates and clears a root <page table>, while `walk()` with allocation enabled allocates and clears missing lower-level tables and installs valid references to them. With allocation disabled, a missing table makes `walk()` return failure. This software routine finds a <page table entry> for the <operating system>; it is not the hardware's complete permission-checking <page table walk>. <xv6 RISC-V source code/vm.c>[walk(): three-level index comment and alloc-controlled creation; uvmcreate(): root allocation and initialization]{ref}

= Page table walk
{parent=Page table}

Looking up <page table entries> to obtain a <page frame> number and effective access attributes. On ordinary <x86> hardware <paging>, a <TLB miss> can initiate this lookup without entering the <operating system>.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Set-user-ID execution
{parent=Operating system}

Execution of a suitable executable with effective user identity set to its owner. A privileged executable writable by untrusted users permits code replacement and privilege escalation.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Protection domain
{parent=Operating system}

A boundary within which code exercises a particular set of permissions. An <address space> describes accessible <virtual addresses>; a <protection domain> also concerns authority over other objects, such as <software threads> and <IPC endpoints>. Two <address spaces> may deliberately share a writable <memory page>, so separation of <address spaces> alone does not make their contents independent.

= User-space service
{parent=Operating system}

An <operating system> service executing at an application's <processor privilege level>, outside the <operating-system kernel>. A <microkernel> can host a <file system> or <device driver> this way. Its granted <security capabilities> and <memory protection> determine its authority; the label <user-space service> does not imply that it has little authority.

= Separation of mechanism and policy
{parent=Operating system}

Distinguishing the operations a system can enforce from the choices about how to use them. In a <microkernel>, a protected <IPC> operation can deliver requests while a <user-space service> decides which <files> a caller may read. The division is a design choice: every real mechanism still has rules and limits.

= Inter-process communication
{parent=Operating system}

= IPC
{synonym}
{c}

Communication between <operating-system processes>, including controlled message delivery and deliberately shared <computer memory>. A <microkernel> supplies <IPC> across <protection domains>, allowing <user-space services> to cooperate without making all their private <computer memory> accessible to each other.

= IPC endpoint
{parent=Inter-process communication}
{c}

A communication object on which <software threads> send or receive <IPC> messages. In <seL4>, an <IPC endpoint> is an <operating-system kernel> object with waiting senders or receivers; access requires a suitable <security capability>. <IPC tutorial by seL4 Project>[IPC tutorial, Background: endpoint queues]{ref}

= Kernel
{parent=Operating system}
{wiki}
{disambiguate=operating system}

= Operating-system kernel
{synonym}

The <operating-system kernel> is the core of an <operating system>, controlling access to resources and supplying mechanisms used by <operating-system processes>. <Processor privilege levels> let it enforce boundaries that ordinary programs cannot simply overwrite. The <operating-system kernel> connects <process scheduling>, <virtual memory> and <file system> operations; a <system call> requests a service across that boundary. A <monolithic kernel> and a <microkernel> divide services differently, so the location of every <file system> service is a design choice.

For the toy <file system>, opening `a` resolves its <directory entry> to <inode> 42, establishes an <open file description>, and returns <file descriptor> `3`. Later reads use that <file descriptor> to reach the existing <open file description>, without resolving the spelling `a` again. This explains how <unlinking an open file> preserves access: the open reference survives the <directory entry>. The per-<operating-system process> table interpreting `3` is maintained by the <operating-system kernel>; `3` itself is the small <integer> passed by the program, not <inode> 42. https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/functions/open.html[The Open Group, POSIX.1-2024, open: file descriptor to open file description to file]{ref}

= System call
{parent=Kernel (operating system)}

A request from a program for an <operating-system kernel> service. In the <Unix> examples here, opening, reading, linking and unlinking <files> request controlled changes or access to <operating system> resources. The <operating-system kernel> checks the request and performs the permitted operation; an interface specified by <POSIX> does not by itself dictate every internal implementation detail.

= Fork
{parent=System call}
{disambiguate=system call}
{wiki}

A <system call> creating a child <operating-system process> from a parent. The <Linux kernel> documents <copy-on-write> for ordinary inherited private <memory pages>, with separate <address spaces> but shared <open file descriptions> for inherited <file descriptors>. <fork(2) — Linux manual page>{ref}

= Linux kernel
{parent=Kernel (operating system)}
{c}

A concrete <operating-system kernel> whose documented <file system> interface distinguishes a name-associated `dentry`, an object-associated `inode`, and a `file` object created for an open instance. An <inode> can have several name-associated objects. This illustrates the distinction among <directory entries>, <inodes> and <open file descriptions>; it does not imply identical structures in every <operating system>. https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/vfs.html[Linux kernel documentation, Overview of the Linux Virtual File System: directory entries, inode objects and file objects]{ref}

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Monolithic kernel
{parent=Kernel (operating system)}

A kernel design in which major operating-system services execute within one privileged address space and can call one another directly. Avoiding cross-domain communication can improve performance, while increasing the trusted code base and the reach of a faulty service.

// Destination: computer-science.bigb

= Microkernel
{parent=Kernel (operating system)}

An <operating-system kernel> design retaining essential protected mechanisms while moving services into <user-space services>. <Address spaces>, <software threads> and <IPC> form a useful small foundation: the <operating-system kernel> protects private state and controls communication, while a <file system> service interprets requests about <files>. This <separation of mechanism and policy> permits different service implementations to use the same protected interface. <On micro-kernel construction by Jochen Liedtke>[Liedtke, On micro-kernel construction, section 2, pp. 238–240: minimality criterion, address spaces, threads and IPC; section 3, pp. 240–241: user-level services]{ref}

The useful question is which authority a service needs. A <device driver> may require access to a device while having no permission to overwrite an unrelated application's <computer memory>. A <microkernel> makes such <protection domains> possible; their actual boundaries depend on the chosen <memory protection> and <security capabilities>.

= Microkernel file-read example
{parent=Microkernel}

Consider an original toy <file system> service in <address space> B and a client in <address space> A, on one <CPU>. The service holds a five-<byte> cached <file> containing `LIME` followed by a newline. The client already has service handle `9`, permission to read that <file>, and a <security capability> to send requests to <IPC endpoint> E. Handle `9` is interpreted by the service; it is neither a <virtual address> nor an <operating-system kernel> object identifier. All relevant <memory pages> are resident, and this read needs no <device driver>.

\Image[microkernel-file-read-message-path.png]
{title=A cached <file> read uses <IPC> between separate <address spaces>, with authority checked by the <microkernel>}

* The client submits `READ(7, 9, 0, 4)`: request identifier `7`, handle `9`, <byte> offset `0`, and requested length `4`. A <system call> enters the <microkernel>; the <security capability> is checked before delivery on <IPC endpoint> E. The client waits for a reply.
* A waiting service <software thread> receives the request. The service checks the handle's read permission and the requested range, then obtains the first four <bytes> from its cached <file>. The <microkernel> transports the request; the service supplies its <file system> meaning.
* The service uses <reply capability> authority to return `OK(7, 4, LIME)`. The client resumes, checks the request identifier and returned length, and copies those four <bytes> into its own local buffer. There were two logical <IPC> messages: request and reply. The four arrows show delivery stages, not four separate requests.

This protocol is an illustrative design, not an actual <seL4> <file system> interface. Its call/reply structure follows <seL4>'s documented <IPC>: `seL4_Call` waits using reply authority, and small payloads can travel in <processor registers>. <IPC tutorial by seL4 Project>[IPC tutorial, Background: System calls; IPC Buffer; Data transfer]{ref}

An ordinary pointer in A refers to A's <address space>. Sending its numeric value does not make B's private <computer memory> appear there. This example explicitly transfers <bytes>. Sharing a mapped <memory page> instead would require intentional <memory protection> permissions and a protocol for ownership and <synchronization>; passing a pointer alone establishes neither.

The same <IPC> mechanism could carry `WRITE`, but this client could still be refused by the service's read-only handle policy. Conversely, possession of an <IPC endpoint> <security capability> permits communication without automatically granting access to every <file> the service manages. The <separation of mechanism and policy> appears twice: endpoint authority gates delivery, and the service's handle policy gates the requested operation.

= Microkernel service failure isolation
{parent=Microkernel}

Suppose the <user-space service> stops after receiving request `7`, before replying. Assume that its private <memory pages> are not writable mappings of the client's or an unrelated service's <computer memory>, and that it lacks <security capabilities> to change their <software threads>. A failed store in the service cannot directly overwrite that private state through ordinary <CPU> <address translation>. Those assumptions establish a particular isolation boundary, not a successful read.

\Image[microkernel-service-failure-authority-boundaries.png]
{title=<Memory protection> and <security capabilities> limit a stopped service's reach; recovery needs an additional design}

The client can remain blocked waiting for request `7`. An unrelated runnable service may continue if <process scheduling> and resources permit, but clients that depend on the stopped service still lose access to its service. A separately authorized recovery manager would need to detect the failure, rebuild the service's state and arrange communication with its replacement. The <microkernel> architecture by itself supplies none of those application-specific recovery decisions.

A missing reply also does not reveal whether a service crashed, is delayed by <process scheduling>, or entered an infinite computation. For a read, retrying after recovery may be straightforward; for a modifying operation, absence of a reply does not prove that the modification never happened. Recovery must account for ambiguous completed operations and any required <file-system crash consistency>.

The boundary changes with authority. A deliberately shared writable <memory page> allows the service to change its shared contents; a <security capability> controlling another <software thread> permits the corresponding control operations. A <device driver> with uncontrolled <DMA> can bypass ordinary <CPU> <memory protection>. Thus service placement and actual permissions must be considered together. <What the Proofs Assume by seL4 Project>[What the Proofs Assume, The Assumptions: DMA and memory-mapped device registers]{ref}

= Microkernel communication cost
{parent=Microkernel}

For the <microkernel file-read example>, distinguish <system call> entry/exit, <security capability> lookup, payload transfer, <process scheduling> and <context switches> from the service's actual <file> lookup and read work. Entering a privileged <processor privilege level> and switching to a different <address space> are different events. A change of <address space> can affect the <translation lookaside buffer>; running different code and touching different <computer memory> can cause <cache misses>. Neither event implies that every <translation lookaside buffer> entry or <cache line> must be discarded.

A larger copied payload requires more transfer work; sharing <memory pages> can avoid copying that bulk data but introduces mapping and <synchronization> work. A service that requests another service adds another <IPC> exchange. If the cached <file> data were absent, communication with a <device driver> and the <input-output> wait would also matter. Blocking behind other requests affects observed response time even when the individual <IPC> operations are cheap.

These are cost sources, not timings or a universal ranking against a <monolithic kernel>. Implementation and <CPU> details matter: the construction analysis separates protected entry costs, <address space> switching, <translation lookaside buffer> effects and <memory cache> effects. <On micro-kernel construction by Jochen Liedtke>[Liedtke, On micro-kernel construction, section 4.1, pp. 241–244: kernel-user switches, address-space switches, and discussion of hardware-dependent cache and TLB effects]{ref}

= seL4
{parent=Microkernel}
{c}

A <microkernel> using <security capabilities> to control access to <operating-system kernel> objects, including <IPC endpoints> and <software threads>. An <IPC endpoint> connects cooperating <user-space services>; its <security capabilities> are distinct from the service-defined handle `9` in the <microkernel file-read example>. <Capabilities tutorial by seL4 Project>[Capabilities tutorial, Background: What is a capability?]{ref}

= seL4 verification scope
{parent=seL4}
{c}

<Formal verification> uses machine-checked <formal proofs> to establish <functional correctness>: the C implementation of verified <seL4> configurations conforms to its <formal specification>. <Binary correctness> connects compiled machine code to that C behaviour where supported. Separate security <formal proofs> cover integrity, resource-access availability and confidentiality under their configuration requirements; these properties are not all available in every configuration. For example, the documented confidentiality theorem separates scheduler security domains rather than arbitrary pairs of <software threads>. <seL4 Proofs by seL4 Project>[seL4 Proofs, High-level Proof Statements: Functional correctness; Binary correctness; Security; System initialisation; platform/configuration qualification]{ref}

The documented assumptions include correct hardware, selected handwritten machine-interface code, correct boot state, <translation lookaside buffer> and <memory cache> management, and noninterfering or trusted <DMA>. The confidentiality result covers information channels in its model; the assumptions page explicitly excludes timing channels from that coverage. <What the Proofs Assume by seL4 Project>[What the Proofs Assume, The Assumptions: Assembly, Hardware, Hardware management, Boot code, DMA, Information side-channels]{ref}

Return to `READ(7, 9, 0, 4)`. Correct <IPC> delivery does not establish that the service looked up the right <file>, returned `LIME`, or ever replied. A service can produce an incorrect payload while the <operating-system kernel> exactly follows its <formal specification>. The <formal proofs> therefore do not automatically prove all <user-space services>, nor turn the resource-access availability theorem into a guarantee that this <file> read completes. The verified foundation supports further <algorithm correctness> and system reasoning; the service protocol and its recovery obligations still need their own justification.

// Destination: computer-science.bigb

= Kernel preemption
{parent=Kernel (operating system)}

Allows a running kernel execution to be involuntarily suspended for another schedulable context. Interrupt delivery and voluntary blocking are separate mechanisms.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Process scheduling
{parent=Operating system}

<Process scheduling> chooses which runnable <operating-system process> or <software thread> uses a <CPU> next, and when it gives up that <CPU>. Runnable means able to execute now; work waiting for an <input-output> operation is not eligible until that wait ends. A <ready queue> records eligible work. The policy chooses a recipient; a <context switch> supplies the mechanism for transferring execution. Sharing a single <CPU> lets a long calculation and an interactive program both make progress, although only one execution stream runs on that <CPU> at an instant.

Consider three single-<software thread> <operating-system processes> on one <CPU>, with abstract time units, zero <context switch> or scheduler overhead, and no <input-output> blocking. Their arrival times and <CPU bursts> are $(a_A,b_A)=(0,5)$, $(a_B,b_B)=(1,3)$ and $(a_C,b_C)=(2,1)$. Each <CPU burst> is all the execution that its task needs. With <FCFS>, the current task runs to completion: A occupies $[0,5)$, B $[5,8)$ and C $[8,9)$. With <round-robin scheduling> and <scheduling quantum> $q=2$, the intervals are A $[0,2)$, B $[2,4)$, C $[4,5)$, A $[5,7)$, B $[7,8)$ and A $[8,9)$. At a boundary, arrivals join the <ready queue> before the unfinished task whose <scheduling quantum> has expired; this fixes the otherwise ambiguous ordering at time 2.

\Image[scheduling-fcfs-versus-round-robin.png]
{title=<FCFS> and <round-robin scheduling> share the same nine units of useful <CPU> work differently}

The following triples always list A, B, C. Completion is an absolute time; <scheduling turnaround time>, <scheduling waiting time> and <scheduling response time> are durations measured from the relevant events.

|| Policy
|| Completion times
|| <Scheduling turnaround times>
|| <Scheduling waiting times>
|| <Scheduling response times>

| <FCFS>
| $(5,8,9)$
| $(5,7,7)$
| $(0,4,6)$
| $(0,4,6)$

| <Round-robin scheduling>, $q=2$
| $(9,8,5)$
| $(9,7,3)$
| $(4,4,2)$
| $(0,1,2)$

For example, B first runs at time 2 under <round-robin scheduling>, so its <scheduling response time> is $2-1=1$. It finishes at time 8, giving <scheduling turnaround time> $8-1=7$ and <scheduling waiting time> $7-3=4$. Under <FCFS>, C waits $8-2=6$ before its sole execution interval. Under <round-robin scheduling>, A waits during $[2,5)$ and $[7,8)$, totaling $3+1=4$. Both timelines do exactly $5+3+1=9$ units of useful <CPU> work; changing the order changes who benefits first.

The appeal is that shared progress can improve responsiveness: B and C get their first <CPU> service sooner under <round-robin scheduling>, while A completes later. The <arithmetic mean> of the <scheduling response times> falls from $10/3$ to $1$; the <arithmetic mean> of the <scheduling turnaround times> is $19/3$ for both policies in this particular example. These are different goals, and this workload establishes no universal ranking. Giving each continuously runnable task repeated turns is a practical form of fairness. A smaller <scheduling quantum> can shorten waits between turns, but makes <context switches> more frequent when competitors remain runnable. If each full slice performs $q$ units of useful work and incurs a fixed switching cost $s$, the overhead fraction in that simplified repeated cycle is $s/(q+s)$. Real costs and workloads determine the choice. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/cpu-sched.pdf[Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and Andrea C. Arpaci-Dusseau, Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces, chapter 7, sections 7.2–7.3 and 7.6–7.7: metrics, FCFS and round robin]{ref}

= Ready queue
{parent=Process scheduling}

A collection of runnable <operating-system processes> or <software threads> waiting for a <CPU>. A running task is not waiting in the <ready queue>, and a task waiting for <input-output> is not runnable. <FCFS> and <round-robin scheduling> can use a <FIFO queue>; other policies can organize eligible work differently, for example with a <priority queue>.

= CPU burst
{parent=Process scheduling}
{c}
{title2=$b_i$}

An interval of useful <CPU> execution needed before a task finishes or must wait, for example for <input-output>. A real <operating-system process> can need many <CPU bursts>; the example under <process scheduling> assigns each task just one <CPU burst>, possibly split across several turns by <round-robin scheduling>.

= Round-robin scheduling
{parent=Process scheduling}

= RR
{c}
{synonym}

Serve the front of a <FIFO queue> of runnable tasks for at most one <scheduling quantum>. If the task finishes or must wait for <input-output>, it leaves the <CPU> earlier; if it remains runnable after consuming its allowance, append it to the rear of the <ready queue>. In the example under <process scheduling>, the <ready queue> at time 2 is B, C, A: B arrived at time 1, C joins at the boundary, then A is requeued. That one convention determines the subsequent order.

= Scheduling quantum
{parent=Round-robin scheduling}
{title2=$q$}

The maximum amount of <CPU> execution allotted in one turn by <round-robin scheduling>. A task can finish before consuming its <scheduling quantum>. When other tasks remain runnable, a shorter <scheduling quantum> provides more frequent turns at the cost of more frequent <context switches>.

= Scheduling preemption
{parent=Process scheduling}

Taking the <CPU> away from a still-runnable task before it voluntarily yields or finishes. <Round-robin scheduling> uses <scheduling preemption> when a <scheduling quantum> expires, commonly using a timer <interrupt> to regain control. <Kernel preemption> is the more specific question of whether an execution already within the <operating-system kernel> can be suspended for another runnable context.

= Scheduling response time
{parent=Process scheduling}
{title2=$R_i=f_i-a_i$}

The delay from a task's arrival $a_i$ until its first execution $f_i$: $R_i=f_i-a_i$. This measures access to initial <CPU> service, rather than completion of an entire user request. For C under <round-robin scheduling> in <process scheduling>, $R_C=4-2=2$; its <scheduling turnaround time> is instead $5-2=3$.

= Scheduling turnaround time
{parent=Process scheduling}
{title2=$T_i=c_i-a_i$}

The duration from a task's arrival $a_i$ until its completion $c_i$: $T_i=c_i-a_i$. It includes execution and all intervening waits. For A under <round-robin scheduling> in <process scheduling>, $T_A=9-0=9$, despite a <scheduling response time> of zero.

= Scheduling waiting time
{parent=Process scheduling}
{title2=$W_i$}

The total duration a task spends runnable but waiting for a <CPU>, including later waits after <scheduling preemption>. Under the zero-overhead, no-<input-output>-blocking assumptions of <process scheduling>, elapsed time partitions into useful execution and such waiting, so $W_i=T_i-b_i$, where $T_i$ is <scheduling turnaround time> and $b_i$ is the total <CPU burst>. Thus C under <round-robin scheduling> has $W_C=3-1=2$. If a task also spends time blocked on <input-output>, that blocked duration belongs to <scheduling turnaround time> but must not be counted as <scheduling waiting time>.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Context switch
{parent=Process scheduling}
{wiki}

A <context switch> transfers <CPU> execution from one <operating-system process> or <software thread> to another while preserving enough <execution context> for the suspended task to resume. The <operating-system kernel> saves the outgoing task's <processor registers>, including its resume <program counter> and <stack pointer>, and restores the incoming task's saved state. Hardware entry and software switching divide this work differently across architectures; the diagram groups them into logical save and load steps. During the transition the same <CPU> executes the <operating-system kernel>'s scheduler and switching code. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/cpu-mechanisms.pdf[Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and Andrea C. Arpaci-Dusseau, Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces, chapter 6, section 6.3: saving and restoring context]{ref}

For a schematic switch from A to B, let A's resume <program counter> be $p_A$, its <stack pointer> $u_A$, and one illustrative data <processor register> contain 7. B's saved values are $p_B,u_B,42$. Saving A retains $(p_A,u_A,7)$ in A's <execution context>; loading B establishes $(p_B,u_B,42)$ before B resumes. These are selected fields, not a complete architecture-specific <execution context>. Later restoring A recovers its 7 even though B has used the same physical <processor register> for 42.

\Image[context-switch-state-transfer.png]
{title=One <CPU> over time: save A's <execution context>, run the scheduler, and restore B's <execution context>}

The useful invariant is that each suspended task retains its own resume state, so another task's use of the <processor registers> does not erase it. This is what makes repeated turns under <round-robin scheduling> possible. Saving an <execution context> does not copy the entire <address space>. Switching between <software threads> of one <operating-system process> can retain the same <address space>; switching between <operating-system processes> with different <address spaces> may also require changing the <memory management unit>'s translation context, such as the active <page table> and associated <address space identifier>, depending on the architecture. Their contents remain in <computer memory>. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/threads-intro.pdf[Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and Andrea C. Arpaci-Dusseau, Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces, chapter 26, introduction: thread contexts and shared address spaces]{ref}

Entering the <operating-system kernel> through an <interrupt>, <synchronous processor exception> or <system call> does not automatically imply a <context switch> to another task. The handler may return to the same task; <process scheduling> determines whether another runnable task should take over. A <context switch> consumes time, and a changed <memory cache> or <translation lookaside buffer> working environment can add subsequent costs. Those costs are excluded from the toy <process scheduling> timelines, which are calculations rather than <operating system> benchmarks. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/cpu-mechanisms.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 6, section 6.3: continue the current process or switch]{ref}https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/cpu-sched.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 7, section 7.7: direct and indirect switching costs]{ref}

= Execution context
{parent=Context switch}

State needed to resume a suspended execution stream, including saved <processor registers> and its position in a <call stack>. An <operating-system kernel> can keep parts in a per-task record and parts on a kernel <call stack>. An <execution context> identifies and preserves that stream's state; it is not a duplicate of all the <computer memory> reachable through its <address space>.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Process
{parent=Operating system}
{wiki}
{disambiguate=computing}

= Operating-system process
{synonym}

An <operating-system process> is an execution instance of a program, with an <address space>, one or more <software threads> carrying <execution contexts>, and <operating system> resources such as <file descriptors>. A program describes instructions; an executable <file> stores a form that can be loaded for execution. Starting the same executable twice can create two <operating-system processes>, each with its own evolving state. A <operating-system process> can be waiting for <input-output> or waiting for a turn on the <CPU>, rather than executing at every instant. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/cpu-intro.pdf[Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and Andrea C. Arpaci-Dusseau, Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces, chapter 4, sections 4.1, 4.3–4.4: program, process and execution states]{ref}

For example, start the same counter program as <operating-system processes> A and B. In this toy mapping, both use <virtual address> `0x1234` for a counter `x`, but A reads `x = 7` while B reads `x = 42`. The number `0x1234` is interpreted through the current <address space>, so it need not denote the same contents in both <operating-system processes>. Writing A's ordinary private counter does not change B's counter. Compare <virtual memory> for an explicit mapping to different <physical addresses>.

\Image[process-and-thread-address-spaces.png]
{title=Separate <operating-system processes> can interpret the same <virtual address> differently; <software threads> in one process share an <address space>}

Separate <virtual memory> <address spaces> provide a usual default isolation boundary, not a claim that all <computer memory> is universally private. Deliberately shared mappings let <operating-system processes> communicate through the same stored data. Even logically private contents need not occupy distinct <page frames> at every moment: read-only program code can be shared, and <copy-on-write> can keep a common <memory page> until a write requires a private copy. The distinction is between which accesses and changes are permitted and which physical storage is currently reused. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/vm-complete.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 23, section 23.1, Other Neat Tricks: copy-on-write and shared mappings]{ref}https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/009695099/functions/shm_open.html[The Open Group, POSIX shared-memory object interface, shm_open: multiple processes can refer to the same shared memory object]{ref}

This abstraction lets independent activities reuse the same executable while keeping their changing state separate. <Process scheduling> distributes <CPU> time; <virtual memory> interprets and protects addresses; <file descriptors> connect an execution to open resources. The useful design is controlled independence with explicit ways to share. Creating <operating-system processes>, maintaining their mappings and performing <context switches> require time and <computer memory>; isolation alone gives no universal performance guarantee.

= File descriptor
{parent=Process (computing)}

A nonnegative <integer> identifying an open access in one <operating-system process>. The program passes its value to operations such as reading and closing; the <operating-system kernel> interprets it through that <operating-system process>'s descriptor table. In the toy example, table entry `3` refers to an <open file description> for <inode> 42. Another <operating-system process>'s entry `3` can reach a different <file>, and a closed <file descriptor> value can be reused. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/file-intro.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 39, section 39.3: per-process file descriptors]{ref}

= Open file description
{parent=File descriptor}

A record of an open access to a <file>, including its current position and access mode. Each <file descriptor> reaches one <open file description>, but several <file descriptors> can share it. Separately opening the same ordinary <file> creates separate <open file descriptions> with independent positions while still reaching the same <inode>. Thus <file descriptors>, <open file descriptions> and <hard links> have different counts. https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/basedefs/V1_chap03.html#tag_03_258[The Open Group, POSIX.1-2017, section 3.258: open file description]{ref}

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Thread
{parent=Process (computing)}
{wiki}
{disambiguate=computing}

= Software thread
{synonym}

A <software thread> is an execution stream within a <operating-system process>. Each <software thread> has its own <execution context>, including <processor registers>, an <instruction pointer>, and a <call stack> selected by its <stack pointer>. <Software threads> in the same <operating-system process> share its <address space>, global variables, dynamically allocated storage (the memory heap), and resources such as <file descriptors>. Separate register states let two <software threads> pause at different instructions and later resume correctly. A <context switch> saves and restores those states; it does not copy the entire shared <address space>. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/threads-intro.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 26, introduction and figure 26.1: thread contexts and shared address space]{ref}https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/basedefs/V1_chap03.html#tag_03_189[The Open Group, POSIX.1-2024, sections 3.189–3.190: Live Process and Live Thread, shared resources and addressable storage]{ref}

In the right half of the diagram under <operating-system process>, T1 and T2 access the same counter `x` at <virtual address> `0x1234`. Their <call stacks> occupy separate regions of the same <address space>, and their saved <processor registers> can contain different values. A <software thread>'s own <call stack> means the stack used for its calls, not a hardware protection boundary against another <software thread>. Another <software thread> with a suitable address can access that stack's live data, subject to the program's lifetime and <synchronization> rules. Sharing an <address space> enables easy communication and makes accidental interference possible.

Concurrency means that several activities can make progress over overlapping intervals; parallel execution means that several activities execute at the same instant. On one <CPU> execution slot, <process scheduling> can interleave <software threads> without simultaneous execution. Multiple processor cores can run eligible <software threads> in parallel, but merely creating <software threads> does not ensure parallel execution or a speedup.

Consider an explicitly abstract interleaving model: shared `x` starts at $0$, and each of T1 and T2 performs three indivisible logical steps in its own order: load `x` into its own register `r`, add $1$ to `r`, then store `r` into `x`. Each load sees the latest preceding store in the displayed order. These model assumptions specify the example; they do not describe every programming language or every hardware memory model. In serial order T1 finishes all three steps before T2 starts, so T2 loads $1$ and stores $2$.

An overlapping order instead gives T1 load $0$, T2 load $0$, T1 add to get `r1 = 1`, T2 add to get `r2 = 1`, T1 store $1$, and T2 store $1$. The load/store subsequence is therefore T1 load $0$, T2 load $0$, T1 store $1$, T2 store $1$: final $x=1$, although each <software thread> incremented its register correctly. The second store replaces the first with the same value, losing one intended update. This <race condition> is possible even with interleaving on a single <CPU>.

\Image[thread-lost-update-interleaving.png]
{title=Two abstract load/add/store increments retain separate registers but can leave the shared counter at $1$ instead of the serial result $2$}

This is not an unprotected C implementation example. Conflicting unsynchronized accesses to an ordinary non-atomic C object from different <software threads> can constitute a data race, which has undefined behavior; the abstract trace is not a prediction of that program's allowed outcomes. https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n1570.pdf[ISO/IEC JTC1/SC22/WG14, committee draft N1570, 12 April 2011, section 5.1.2.4 paragraph 25: data races and undefined behavior]{ref}

The repair explains why <synchronization> matters: <mutual exclusion> around the whole load/add/store sequence prevents the other <software thread> from starting an increment halfway through it. Alternatively, an appropriate <atomic operation> can make the complete increment one indivisible read-modify-write. In the toy model, either repair makes the two increments take effect in an order, producing $0+1+1=2$. Making only each load and each store an <atomic operation> still permits the displayed lost update. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/threads-locks.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 28, sections 28.1–28.2 and 28.11: locks around updates and atomic fetch-and-add]{ref}

The attraction is independently progressing activities with direct access to shared data: a user-interface <software thread> can handle input while another waits for <input-output>, and workers can divide independent pieces of a large calculation among available cores. The first benefit can exist on one <CPU>; the second requires suitable work and execution resources. <Context switches>, <synchronization>, register-state records and extra <call stacks> consume time or <computer memory>, so the useful partition depends on the workload. https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/threads-intro.pdf[Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, OSTEP chapter 26, section 26.1: parallel work and overlapping input-output with other activity]{ref}

= Race condition
{parent=Thread (computing)}

A dependence of behavior on the relative timing or ordering of concurrent activities. In the abstract example under <software thread>, two increments can leave $x=1$ or $x=2$ depending on their interleaving. A C data race is a language-defined conflict with undefined behavior, rather than a name for every <race condition>.

= Synchronization
{parent=Thread (computing)}
{disambiguate=computing}

= Synchronization
{synonym}

Coordinating concurrent activities so that required ordering and visibility rules hold. <Mutual exclusion> is one form of <synchronization>; waiting for another <software thread> to finish is another.

= Mutual exclusion
{parent=Synchronization (computing)}

<Mutual exclusion> allows at most one participating <software thread> at a time to execute a protected <critical section>. A <mutex> is an object used to enforce that rule; the <critical section> is the region of the program that needs the rule. Several different <critical sections> can use the same <mutex> to protect the same shared data. The benefit is reasoning about a complete update without another cooperating <software thread> observing or changing its intermediate state.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Locks>[pp. 1–2: lock ownership and critical sections]{ref}

For a small <lost update>, shared `x` initially equals $0$. Each of two <software threads>, T1 and T2, uses its own local `r` and performs these three steps:
```
r = read(x)
r = r + 1
write(x, r)
```
Assume <sequential consistency>: each step is indivisible, each <software thread> keeps the displayed order, and a read sees the latest preceding write to `x`. In the <thread interleaving> T1 read, T2 read, T1 add, T2 add, T1 write, T2 write, both local values become $1$ and both writes store $1$. Final $x=1$ instead of $2$: a <lost update> despite two correctly executed local additions. Separate indivisible reads and writes are insufficient; the complete read, modify and write must be protected.

\Image[mutual-exclusion-lost-update-timelines.png]
{title=A <lost update> and its repair by <mutual exclusion> around the complete increment}

The <binomial coefficient> $\binom{6}{3}=20$ counts the <thread interleavings> preserving the two three-step orders: choose T1's three positions, with the remaining positions belonging to T2. Exhaustive enumeration gives final $x=1$ in 18 orders and $x=2$ in the two serial orders. These counts are not probabilities: no random scheduler has been specified. With the same <mutex> acquired before reading and released after writing, only the two complete increment orders remain, and both give $2$. Alternatively, an <atomic read-modify-write> increment updates `x` in one indivisible step. Protecting only the write still allows both <software threads> to read $0$ beforehand.

<Mutual exclusion> is a <safety property>: two participants must never be inside the protected <critical section> together. Eventual entry for every persistent requester is a <liveness property>, expressed here as <starvation freedom>. A protocol that never admits anyone satisfies the first requirement while failing the second. Nor does a <mutex> alone promise which waiting <software thread> enters next, or that a producer runs before a consumer. Such application ordering needs an additional <synchronization> protocol.<Defining Liveness by Bowen Alpern and others>[pp. 181–182: mutual exclusion, finite witnesses to safety violations, and eventual service]{ref}

The <sequential consistency> assumptions delimit this calculation. In C++, unsynchronized conflicting accesses to an ordinary shared object can be a <data race>, which makes behavior undefined; the 20 schedules are not a prediction for that program. An <atomic operation> with relaxed ordering also supplies no <acquire-release synchronization> for other data. A real <mutex> must supply both exclusive entry and the required <happens-before> ordering between its users.<Working Draft, Standard for Programming Language C++ (N4861)>{ref}

= Critical section
{parent=Mutual exclusion}
{wiki}

A region whose accesses must obey a shared <synchronization> protocol. For the <lost update> in <mutual exclusion>, the <critical section> includes the read and the local addition as well as the write. Choosing its boundary means identifying the complete operation whose intermediate state must be protected. Every conflicting access must participate in a compatible protocol; merely declaring a <mutex> does not protect accesses that bypass it.

= Mutex
{parent=Mutual exclusion}

An object with acquire and release operations that provides exclusive ownership to a participating <software thread>. Several source-code regions can acquire the same <mutex>; two different <mutex> objects do not exclude each other's users. The caller should enter its <critical section> only after successful acquisition and release ownership on every exit path. A <mutex> may make a waiting <software thread> sleep or use a <spinlock>; the interface and the waiting mechanism are distinct.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Locks>{ref}

= Spinlock
{parent=Mutex}
{wiki}

A mechanism that waits for ownership by repeatedly trying while consuming <CPU> execution time. A <spinlock> built from <test-and-set> can enforce <mutual exclusion> without guaranteeing <starvation freedom>. Progress also requires the owner to run and release: if a waiting <software thread> monopolizes the only <CPU> while the owner is suspended, exclusive entry alone cannot help.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Locks>[pp. 8–9: preemption requirement and lack of fairness guarantees]{ref}

= Test-and-set
{parent=Spinlock}
{wiki}

An <atomic read-modify-write> that returns a flag's old value while setting it. In a <sequential consistency> model with the flag initially $0$, `exchange(flag, 1)` permits entry only when it returns $0$; an owner resets the flag to $0$ on release. Between releases only one exchange can observe $0$. By contrast, separate `read(flag)` and `write(flag, 1)` steps allow both contenders to read $0$ before either writes $1$. The indivisibility of the exchange is an assumed primitive, not something proved by writing its steps as ordinary assignments.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Locks>{ref}

= Thread interleaving
{parent=Mutual exclusion}

An ordered execution obtained by mixing steps of several <software threads> while retaining each one's own step order. In the <sequential consistency> model of <mutual exclusion>, an indivisible step finishes before the next begins. Selecting the step boundaries is part of the model: an <atomic read-modify-write> increment has one boundary, whereas a separate read, local addition and write have three. Enumerating all such orders checks this specified model, not an arbitrary <memory model> or a real <mutex> implementation.

= Lost update
{parent=Thread interleaving}

A change that is overwritten by another update based on an older value. In <mutual exclusion>, both <software threads> read $0$ and later write $1$, so one intended increment is lost. This <race condition> can occur with individually atomic reads and writes and therefore need not involve a <data race>. Its repair must cover the complete update, using a <critical section> or an appropriate <atomic read-modify-write>.

= Safety property
{parent=Synchronization (computing)}

A requirement whose violation has a finite execution prefix that no continuation can repair. Two <software threads> simultaneously inside a protected <critical section> already violate <mutual exclusion>; later leaving does not erase that violation. This contrasts with a <liveness property>, for which a long finite wait alone does not establish failure.<Defining Liveness by Bowen Alpern and others>[pp. 181–182: finite irremediable prefixes]{ref}

= Liveness property
{parent=Synchronization (computing)}

A requirement of eventual progress, with no finite prefix ruling out its eventual satisfaction. For <starvation freedom>, a persistent requester must eventually enter its <critical section>. Establishing such a <liveness property> requires explicit scheduling and protocol assumptions in addition to <mutual exclusion>. Exhaustive finite <thread interleaving> checks alone do not establish eventual service for repeatedly competing <software threads>.<Defining Liveness by Bowen Alpern and others>[p. 182: every finite prefix can still be extended to satisfy a liveness property]{ref}

= Starvation freedom
{parent=Liveness property}

For acquisition of a <mutex>, the guarantee that every persistent requester eventually succeeds, under specified scheduling assumptions and with owners eventually releasing. Some <software threads> repeatedly succeeding while another waits forever violates <starvation freedom> without violating <mutual exclusion>. A finite bound on the wait, and service in arrival order, are additional requirements.

= Deadlock
{parent=Synchronization (computing)}
{wiki}

A group of participants cannot proceed because each waits for a resource whose release depends on another participant in the group. Consider two initially free <mutex> objects A and B. T1 acquires A then tries B; T2 acquires B then tries A. If each obtains its first <mutex>, both wait permanently for the other, with release reachable only after the second acquisition. Each <mutex> still enforces <mutual exclusion>.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Common Concurrency Problems>[pp. 5–7, figures 32.6–32.7: opposing acquisition orders and circular wait]{ref}

\Image[mutual-exclusion-lock-order-state-space.png]
{title=Reachable states for opposite <mutex> acquisition orders and for one shared <lock ordering>}

Each <software thread> performs four indivisible operations: acquire its first <mutex>, acquire its second, release the second, release the first. An unavailable acquisition blocks; neither participant releases early. A state records each participant's completed-operation count and both owners. Exhaustive exploration finds 19 reachable states with opposite orders, including exactly one nonterminal state with no enabled operation: both counts are $1$, T1 owns A, and T2 owns B. Giving both participants the order A then B leaves 16 reachable states and no such blocked state. Unshown grid positions are unreachable. These are tiny <sequential consistency> models, without timeouts, cancellation, recursive acquisition or other resources.

= Deadlock freedom
{parent=Deadlock}

Absence of a reachable <deadlock>. In the finite acquisition model under <deadlock>, reaching a nonterminal state with no enabled operation is a finite witness to failure, so this formulation is a <safety property>. Sometimes the phrase is used for eventual acquisition by some requester, a <liveness property>; the intended requirement must be stated. Neither formulation by itself promises <starvation freedom> for every requester.

= Lock ordering
{parent=Deadlock}

A shared order governing acquisition of distinct <mutex> objects. To prevent circular waiting, choose a strict <total order> and acquire only a higher-ranked <mutex> while retaining lower-ranked ones. In the two-object <deadlock> example, every participant acquires A before B. An outline of the argument is that ranks would strictly increase around any alleged waiting cycle, yet return to the starting rank, a contradiction. The rule must include acquisitions hidden inside called routines. It excludes this kind of <deadlock>, not arbitrary waiting on other resources or failure to schedule an owner.<Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and others/Common Concurrency Problems>[pp. 7–8: consistent acquisition ordering and hidden calls]{ref}

= Memory model
{parent=Synchronization (computing)}

Rules specifying which shared-data observations and operation orders are allowed. <Sequential consistency> is the model used in the two diagrams. A language <memory model> can permit weaker ordering and impose additional conditions on conflicting accesses. Correct use of an indivisible flag must therefore be checked against that <memory model>, including <happens-before> relationships for the data guarded by the flag.

= Sequential consistency
{parent=Memory model}
{wiki}

Execution behaves as if steps occurred in one <total order> preserving each participant's program order; reads observe the latest preceding writes in that order. This is the definition given in the 1979 paper https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2016/12/How-to-Make-a-Multiprocessor-Computer-That-Correctly-Executes-Multiprocess-Programs.pdf[How to Make a Multiprocessor Computer That Correctly Executes Multiprocess Programs], printed p. 690. It permits many <thread interleavings>, including the <lost update> under <mutual exclusion>; ordering individual accesses does not combine them into one <atomic operation>.

= Data race
{parent=Memory model}

For the C++ case used here, conflicting accesses by different <software threads>, at least one non-atomic, with neither ordered before the other by <happens-before>. Such a <data race> has undefined behavior. A <race condition> is broader: the <lost update> with separate atomic reads and writes depends on their order while avoiding a <data race>.<Working Draft, Standard for Programming Language C++ (N4861)>[pp. 79–80: conflicting actions and undefined behavior]{ref}

= Happens-before
{parent=Memory model}
{title2=$\to_{\mathrm{hb}}$}

An ordering <binary relation> used by a <memory model> to constrain observation of shared data. For the <mutex> example, T1's write precedes its release, that release synchronizes with T2's successful acquisition, and that acquisition precedes T2's read. Together these steps order the write before the read. Wall-clock timing alone does not establish <happens-before>.<Working Draft, Standard for Programming Language C++ (N4861)>{ref}

= Acquire-release synchronization
{parent=Happens-before}

A release on an atomic object can synchronize with an acquire that reads the released value, establishing <happens-before> for surrounding accesses. A <test-and-set> <spinlock> needs suitable acquisition and release semantics for its guarded data; relaxed flag operations alone do not supply that relationship. This is a separate question from the flag's indivisibility.<Working Draft, Standard for Programming Language C++ (N4861)>[pp. 1524–1525: relaxed ordering and release/acquire synchronization]{ref}

= Atomic operation
{parent=Synchronization (computing)}

An operation that takes effect indivisibly relative to the specified competing accesses. An atomic increment protects the whole read-modify-write; separate atomic loads and stores do not make their combined sequence an <atomic operation>.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Atomic read-modify-write
{parent=Atomic operation}

An <atomic operation> that reads a shared object's value and changes it as one indivisible action. For an increment from $0$, the two operations under <mutual exclusion> produce $1$ then $2$ in either <software thread> order. A <test-and-set> is another example. The operation's ordering guarantees for other data still depend on the <memory model> and its selected <acquire-release synchronization> semantics.
